1
00:02:02,837 --> 00:02:04,797
Mr. Dufresne, describe...

2
00:02:05,006 --> 00:02:09,344
...the confrontation you had with your wife the night she was murdered.

3
00:02:14,015 --> 00:02:15,683
It was very bitter.

4
00:02:16,017 --> 00:02:20,355
She said she was glad I knew, that she hated all the sneaking around.

5
00:02:21,898 --> 00:02:25,401
And she said that she wanted a divorce in Reno.

6
00:02:25,652 --> 00:02:29,280
- What was your response? - I told her I would not grant one.

7
00:02:29,488 --> 00:02:32,658
"I'll see you in hell before I see you in Reno."

8
00:02:32,867 --> 00:02:36,204
Those were your words, according to your neighbors.

9
00:02:37,287 --> 00:02:38,915
If they say so.

10
00:02:39,415 --> 00:02:41,960
I really don't remember. I was upset.

11
00:02:42,209 --> 00:02:44,670
What happened after you argued with your wife?

12
00:02:45,839 --> 00:02:47,590
She packed a bag.

13
00:02:48,758 --> 00:02:51,928
She packed a bag to go and stay with Mr. Quentin.

14
00:02:52,554 --> 00:02:56,640
Glenn Quentin, golf pro at the Snowden Hills Country Club...

15
00:02:56,850 --> 00:02:59,977
...whom you had discovered was your wife's lover.

16
00:03:02,313 --> 00:03:03,481
Did you follow her?

17
00:03:04,857 --> 00:03:06,943
I went to a few bars first.

18
00:03:07,151 --> 00:03:11,530
Later, I drove to his house to confront them. They weren't home.

19
00:03:12,072 --> 00:03:13,616
I parked in the turnout...

20
00:03:14,325 --> 00:03:15,284
...and waited.

21
00:03:15,535 --> 00:03:17,160
With what intention?

22
00:03:17,787 --> 00:03:19,204
I'm not sure.

23
00:03:20,373 --> 00:03:21,833
I was confused...

24
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:23,083
...drunk.

25
00:03:24,586 --> 00:03:25,879
I think...

26
00:03:26,086 --> 00:03:28,173
...mostly I wanted to scare them.

27
00:03:28,422 --> 00:03:31,843
When they arrived, you went up to the house and murdered them.

28
00:03:32,759 --> 00:03:34,137
I was sobering up.

29
00:03:34,344 --> 00:03:38,016
I got back in the car and I drove home to sleep it off.

30
00:03:38,223 --> 00:03:41,186
Along the way, I threw my gun into the Royal River.

31
00:03:41,393 --> 00:03:43,353
I've been very clear on this point.

32
00:03:43,603 --> 00:03:47,692
I get hazy where the cleaning woman shows up the following morning...

33
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:50,485
...and finds your wife in bed with her lover...

34
00:03:50,694 --> 00:03:52,780
...riddled with.38-caliber bullets.

35
00:03:54,156 --> 00:03:58,369
Does that strike you as a fantastic coincidence, or is it just me?

36
00:03:59,245 --> 00:04:00,246
Yes, it does.

37
00:04:00,454 --> 00:04:03,624
Yet you still maintain you threw your gun into the river...

38
00:04:03,832 --> 00:04:05,876
...before the murders took place.

39
00:04:06,084 --> 00:04:08,337
That's very convenient.

40
00:04:08,545 --> 00:04:10,214
It's the truth.

41
00:04:10,589 --> 00:04:15,136
The police dragged that river for three days, and nary a gun was found...

42
00:04:15,344 --> 00:04:19,430
...so no comparison could be made between your gun and the bullets...

43
00:04:19,641 --> 00:04:23,102
...taken from the bloodstained corpses of the victims.

44
00:04:23,769 --> 00:04:26,146
And that also...

45
00:04:26,605 --> 00:04:30,067
...is very convenient. Isn't it, Mr. Dufresne?

46
00:04:31,401 --> 00:04:33,445
Since I am innocent of this crime...

47
00:04:33,654 --> 00:04:38,283
...I find it decidedly inconvenient that the gun was never found.

48
00:04:56,176 --> 00:04:59,555
Ladies and gentlemen, you've heard all the evidence.

49
00:04:59,763 --> 00:05:03,600
We have the accused at the scene of the crime. We have footprints.

50
00:05:03,809 --> 00:05:06,645
Bullets on the ground bearing his fingerprints.

51
00:05:06,854 --> 00:05:09,815
A broken bourbon bottle, likewise with fingerprints.

52
00:05:10,023 --> 00:05:11,608
And most of all...

53
00:05:11,817 --> 00:05:14,862
...we have a beautiful young woman and her lover...

54
00:05:15,070 --> 00:05:17,113
...lying dead in each other's arms.

55
00:05:18,282 --> 00:05:20,909
They had sinned.

56
00:05:22,119 --> 00:05:24,620
But was their crime so great...

57
00:05:24,912 --> 00:05:27,124
...as to merit a death sentence?

58
00:05:27,332 --> 00:05:29,626
While you think about that...

59
00:05:31,044 --> 00:05:32,838
...think about this:

60
00:05:33,713 --> 00:05:37,466
A revolver holds six bullets, not eight.

61
00:05:37,676 --> 00:05:41,346
I submit that this was not a hot-blooded crime of passion.

62
00:05:41,555 --> 00:05:44,349
That at least could be understood, if not condoned.

63
00:05:44,557 --> 00:05:45,601
No.

64
00:05:45,808 --> 00:05:46,935
This was revenge...

65
00:05:47,143 --> 00:05:51,565
...of a much more brutal, cold-blooded nature. Consider this:

66
00:05:51,772 --> 00:05:54,151
Four bullets per victim.

67
00:05:54,902 --> 00:05:56,653
Not six shots fired, but eight.

68
00:05:57,154 --> 00:06:00,740
That means that he fired the gun empty...

69
00:06:00,991 --> 00:06:02,909
...and then stopped to reload...

70
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:06,622
...so that he could shoot each of them again.

71
00:06:06,830 --> 00:06:09,373
An extra bullet per lover...

72
00:06:10,167 --> 00:06:11,919
...right in the head.

73
00:06:15,422 --> 00:06:20,218
You strike me as a particularly icy and remorseless man, Mr. Dufresne.

74
00:06:20,636 --> 00:06:22,763
It chills my blood just to look at you.

75
00:06:24,722 --> 00:06:27,475
By the power vested in me by the state of Maine...

76
00:06:27,684 --> 00:06:31,938
...I hereby order you to serve two life sentences back-to-back...

77
00:06:32,314 --> 00:06:34,815
...one for each of your victims. So be it!

78
00:06:59,966 --> 00:07:01,133
Sit.

79
00:07:05,681 --> 00:07:08,516
We see you've served 20 years of a life sentence?

80
00:07:09,434 --> 00:07:12,645
- Yes, sir. - You feel you've been rehabilitated?

81
00:07:13,062 --> 00:07:15,648
Yes, sir. Absolutely, sir.

82
00:07:16,775 --> 00:07:18,776
I mean, I learned my lesson.

83
00:07:19,861 --> 00:07:23,282
I can honestly say that I'm a changed man.

84
00:07:26,534 --> 00:07:28,912
I'm no longer a danger to society.

85
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:31,081
That's God's honest truth.

86
00:08:07,366 --> 00:08:08,659
Hey, Red.

87
00:08:08,868 --> 00:08:10,161
How'd it go?

88
00:08:10,368 --> 00:08:12,537
Same old shit, different day.

89
00:08:12,830 --> 00:08:14,665
Yeah, I know how you feel.

90
00:08:14,874 --> 00:08:16,875
I'm up for rejection next week.

91
00:08:17,668 --> 00:08:20,171
Yeah, I got rejected last week.

92
00:08:20,379 --> 00:08:21,672
It happens.

93
00:08:21,881 --> 00:08:23,341
Hey, Red, bump me a deck.

94
00:08:23,507 --> 00:08:26,802
Get out of my face, man! You're into me for five packs already.

95
00:08:27,053 --> 00:08:28,012
Four!

96
00:08:29,180 --> 00:08:32,600
There must be a con like me in every prison in America.

97
00:08:32,808 --> 00:08:34,602
I'm the guy who can get it for you.

98
00:08:34,809 --> 00:08:37,897
Cigarettes, a bag of reefer, if that's your thing...

99
00:08:38,104 --> 00:08:41,525
...bottle of brandy to celebrate your kid's high school graduation.

100
00:08:41,734 --> 00:08:43,861
Damn near anything within reason.

101
00:08:44,070 --> 00:08:47,823
Yes, sir! I'm a regular Sears and Roebuck.

102
00:08:50,743 --> 00:08:53,703
So when Andy Dufresne came to me in 1949...

103
00:08:53,871 --> 00:08:57,457
...and asked me to smuggle Rita Hayworth into the prison for him...

104
00:08:57,667 --> 00:08:59,918
...I told him, "No problem."

105
00:09:41,793 --> 00:09:43,962
Andy came to Shawshank Prison...

106
00:09:44,213 --> 00:09:48,049
...in early 1947 for murdering his wife and the fella she was banging.

107
00:09:48,425 --> 00:09:52,470
On the outside, he'd been vice president of a large Portland bank.

108
00:09:52,929 --> 00:09:54,724
Good work for a man so young.

109
00:10:37,974 --> 00:10:39,726
You speak English, butt-steak?

110
00:10:39,936 --> 00:10:41,770
You follow this officer.

111
00:10:54,534 --> 00:10:58,787
I never seen such a sorry-looking heap of maggot shit in all my life.

112
00:10:59,037 --> 00:11:01,623
Hey, fish! Come over here!

113
00:11:24,896 --> 00:11:26,313
Taking bets today, Red?

114
00:11:26,690 --> 00:11:28,358
Smokes or coin? Bettor's choice.

115
00:11:28,567 --> 00:11:30,277
Smokes. Put me down for two.

116
00:11:30,819 --> 00:11:32,361
All right, who's your horse?

117
00:11:32,571 --> 00:11:34,322
That little sack of shit.

118
00:11:35,949 --> 00:11:39,202
- Eighth. He'll be first. - Bullshit! I'll take that action.

119
00:11:39,411 --> 00:11:41,620
You're out some smokes, son.

120
00:11:41,830 --> 00:11:43,582
If you're so smart, you call it.

121
00:11:43,832 --> 00:11:46,877
I'll take that chubby fat-ass there.

122
00:11:47,085 --> 00:11:49,671
The fifth one. Put me down for a quarter deck.

123
00:11:52,882 --> 00:11:54,593
Fresh fish today!

124
00:11:55,385 --> 00:11:56,845
We're reeling them in!

125
00:11:57,511 --> 00:12:01,183
I admit I didn't think much of Andy first time I laid eyes on him.

126
00:12:01,725 --> 00:12:04,602
Looked like a stiff breeze would blow him over.

127
00:12:04,936 --> 00:12:07,062
That was my first impression of the man.

128
00:12:07,272 --> 00:12:08,732
What do you say?

129
00:12:08,898 --> 00:12:12,194
That tall drink of water with the silver spoon up his ass.

130
00:12:12,401 --> 00:12:14,528
That guy? Never happen.

131
00:12:14,738 --> 00:12:17,031
-10 cigarettes. - That's a rich bet.

132
00:12:17,240 --> 00:12:19,284
Who's going to prove me wrong?

133
00:12:19,492 --> 00:12:21,286
Heywood? Jigger?

134
00:12:21,578 --> 00:12:22,871
Skeets?

135
00:12:23,078 --> 00:12:24,414
Floyd!

136
00:12:25,372 --> 00:12:27,416
Four brave souls.

137
00:12:27,626 --> 00:12:30,378
Return to your cellblocks for evening count.

138
00:12:30,586 --> 00:12:33,881
All prisoners, return to your cellblocks.

139
00:12:54,276 --> 00:12:55,402
Turn to the right!

140
00:12:55,611 --> 00:12:56,904
Eyes front.

141
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,586
This is Mr. Hadley. He's captain of the guards.

142
00:13:12,795 --> 00:13:14,922
I'm Mr. Norton, the warden.

143
00:13:15,131 --> 00:13:17,300
You are convicted felons.

144
00:13:17,633 --> 00:13:19,593
That's why they've sent you to me.

145
00:13:20,177 --> 00:13:21,595
Rule number one:

146
00:13:21,805 --> 00:13:23,264
No blasphemy.

147
00:13:23,807 --> 00:13:27,226
I'll not have the Lord's name taken in vain in my prison.

148
00:13:27,434 --> 00:13:28,602
The other rules...

149
00:13:28,811 --> 00:13:32,106
...you'll figure out as you go along. Any questions?

150
00:13:32,482 --> 00:13:33,566
When do we eat?

151
00:13:42,241 --> 00:13:44,243
You eat when we say you eat.

152
00:13:44,493 --> 00:13:47,746
You shit when we say you shit, and piss when we say you piss.

153
00:13:47,955 --> 00:13:50,790
You got that, you maggot-dick motherfucker?

154
00:13:52,668 --> 00:13:54,004
On your feet.

155
00:13:54,211 --> 00:13:55,963
I believe in two things:

156
00:13:56,173 --> 00:13:58,674
Discipline and the Bible.

157
00:13:59,175 --> 00:14:01,217
Here, you'll receive both.

158
00:14:02,011 --> 00:14:04,138
Put your trust in the Lord.

159
00:14:04,722 --> 00:14:06,974
Your ass belongs to me.

160
00:14:08,851 --> 00:14:10,728
Welcome to Shawshank.

161
00:14:15,900 --> 00:14:17,276
Unhook them.

162
00:14:22,698 --> 00:14:24,074
Turn around.

163
00:14:30,915 --> 00:14:32,333
That's enough.

164
00:14:33,250 --> 00:14:35,252
Move to the end of the cage.

165
00:14:36,253 --> 00:14:38,797
Turn around. Delouse him.

166
00:14:40,925 --> 00:14:42,176
Turn around.

167
00:14:45,054 --> 00:14:47,722
Move out of the cage. Pick up your clothes and Bible.

168
00:14:47,932 --> 00:14:49,266
Next man up!

169
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:53,020
To the right.

170
00:14:53,228 --> 00:14:54,271
Right. Right.

171
00:14:54,855 --> 00:14:56,065
Left.

172
00:14:56,482 --> 00:15:00,276
The first night's the toughest. No doubt about it.

173
00:15:00,735 --> 00:15:03,864
They march you in naked as the day you were born...

174
00:15:04,072 --> 00:15:07,868
...skin burning and half-blind from that delousing shit.

175
00:15:08,535 --> 00:15:10,913
And when they put you in that cell...

176
00:15:11,120 --> 00:15:13,456
...and those bars slam home...

177
00:15:13,916 --> 00:15:16,126
...that's when you know it's for real.

178
00:15:16,751 --> 00:15:19,755
Old life blown away in the blink of an eye.

179
00:15:20,255 --> 00:15:24,343
Nothing left but all the time in the world to think about it.

180
00:15:25,426 --> 00:15:28,806
Most new fish come close to madness the first night.

181
00:15:29,139 --> 00:15:32,100
Somebody always breaks down crying.

182
00:15:32,601 --> 00:15:34,227
Happens every time.

183
00:15:35,145 --> 00:15:36,939
The only question is...

184
00:15:37,146 --> 00:15:38,898
...who's it going to be?

185
00:15:40,316 --> 00:15:43,279
It's as good a thing to bet on as any, I guess.

186
00:15:44,320 --> 00:15:46,990
I had my money on Andy Dufresne.

187
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:57,667
Lights out!

188
00:16:18,188 --> 00:16:20,524
I remember my first night.

189
00:16:21,357 --> 00:16:24,319
Seems like a long time ago.

190
00:16:27,530 --> 00:16:29,115
Hey, fish.

191
00:16:30,158 --> 00:16:31,952
Fish, fish.

192
00:16:33,537 --> 00:16:35,288
What are you, scared of the dark?

193
00:16:37,665 --> 00:16:40,335
Bet you wish your daddy never dicked your mama!

194
00:16:40,543 --> 00:16:44,005
Piggy! Pork! I want me a pork chop.

195
00:16:52,347 --> 00:16:55,558
The boys always go fishing with first-timers.

196
00:16:55,892 --> 00:16:58,853
And they don't quit till they reel someone in.

197
00:17:00,230 --> 00:17:01,814
Hey, Fat Ass.

198
00:17:03,358 --> 00:17:04,651
Fat Ass!

199
00:17:05,859 --> 00:17:07,569
Talk to me, boy.

200
00:17:08,570 --> 00:17:10,990
I know you're there. I can hear you breathing.

201
00:17:11,199 --> 00:17:14,160
Don't you listen to these nitwits, you hear me?

202
00:17:14,368 --> 00:17:16,246
This place ain't so bad.

203
00:17:16,704 --> 00:17:17,914
Tell you what...

204
00:17:19,207 --> 00:17:22,292
...I'll introduce you around, make you feel right at home.

205
00:17:22,877 --> 00:17:25,839
I know a couple of big old bull queers that'd just...

206
00:17:26,089 --> 00:17:28,007
...love to make your acquaintance.

207
00:17:28,215 --> 00:17:31,719
Especially that big, white, mushy butt of yours.

208
00:17:31,928 --> 00:17:33,012
God!

209
00:17:33,721 --> 00:17:35,098
I don't belong here!

210
00:17:35,306 --> 00:17:38,142
- We have a winner! - I want to go home!

211
00:17:38,392 --> 00:17:41,062
And it's Fat Ass by a nose!

212
00:17:43,898 --> 00:17:45,607
Fresh fish!

213
00:17:51,739 --> 00:17:53,532
I want to go home!

214
00:17:54,534 --> 00:17:55,743
I want my mother!

215
00:17:56,035 --> 00:17:58,204
I had your mother! She wasn't that great!

216
00:18:02,584 --> 00:18:04,543
What the Christ is this horseshit?

217
00:18:04,752 --> 00:18:06,880
He blasphemed. I'll tell the warden.

218
00:18:07,088 --> 00:18:09,464
You'll tell him with my baton up your ass!

219
00:18:09,674 --> 00:18:10,508
Let me out!

220
00:18:10,716 --> 00:18:14,094
What is your malfunction, you fat barrel of monkey spunk?

221
00:18:14,304 --> 00:18:17,307
Please! I ain't supposed to be here.

222
00:18:17,806 --> 00:18:18,892
Not me!

223
00:18:19,099 --> 00:18:21,394
I won't count to three. Not even to one.

224
00:18:21,602 --> 00:18:23,688
You shut up, or I'll sing you a lullaby!

225
00:18:24,146 --> 00:18:26,899
Shut up, man. Shut up!

226
00:18:27,609 --> 00:18:30,277
You don't understand. I'm not supposed to be here.

227
00:18:30,486 --> 00:18:31,779
Open that cell.

228
00:18:31,987 --> 00:18:35,241
Me neither! They run this place like a fucking prison!

229
00:18:43,123 --> 00:18:44,751
Son of a bitch!

230
00:18:49,672 --> 00:18:52,008
Captain, take it easy!

231
00:19:14,404 --> 00:19:17,658
If I hear so much as a mouse fart in here tonight...

232
00:19:17,866 --> 00:19:21,328
...I swear by God and Jesus, you will all visit the infirmary.

233
00:19:21,829 --> 00:19:24,122
Every last motherfucker in here.

234
00:19:27,543 --> 00:19:31,213
Call the trustees. Take that tub of shit down to the infirmary.

235
00:19:45,561 --> 00:19:50,191
His first night in the joint, Dufresne cost me two packs of cigarettes.

236
00:19:50,399 --> 00:19:53,402
He never made a sound.

237
00:20:08,750 --> 00:20:11,086
Tier 3 north, clear count!

238
00:20:12,003 --> 00:20:13,839
Tier 2 north, clear count.

239
00:20:14,339 --> 00:20:16,675
Tier 3 south, clear.

240
00:20:21,555 --> 00:20:23,098
Prepare to roll out.

241
00:20:26,726 --> 00:20:28,186
Roll out!

242
00:21:22,115 --> 00:21:24,284
Are you going to eat that?

243
00:21:24,951 --> 00:21:27,245
I hadn't planned on it.

244
00:21:28,287 --> 00:21:29,539
Do you mind?

245
00:21:38,131 --> 00:21:39,507
That's nice and ripe.

246
00:21:51,269 --> 00:21:53,313
Jake says thank you.

247
00:21:53,813 --> 00:21:56,983
Fell out of his nest over by the plate shop.

248
00:21:58,442 --> 00:22:01,779
I'm going to look after him until he's big enough to fly.

249
00:22:03,282 --> 00:22:04,741
Oh, no! Here he comes.

250
00:22:08,078 --> 00:22:09,496
Morning, fellas.

251
00:22:09,745 --> 00:22:11,665
Fine morning, isn't it?

252
00:22:11,998 --> 00:22:14,250
You know why it's a fine morning, don't you?

253
00:22:14,500 --> 00:22:16,752
Send them down. I want them lined up...

254
00:22:17,003 --> 00:22:19,589
...just like a pretty little chorus line.

255
00:22:20,673 --> 00:22:21,883
Look at that.

256
00:22:22,425 --> 00:22:25,595
- I can't stand this guy. - Oh, Lord!

257
00:22:26,263 --> 00:22:28,724
Yes! Richmond, Virginia.

258
00:22:28,931 --> 00:22:30,309
Smell my ass!

259
00:22:30,516 --> 00:22:32,477
After he smells mine.

260
00:22:32,935 --> 00:22:36,647
That's a shame about your horse coming in last and all.

261
00:22:36,856 --> 00:22:39,650
But I sure do love that winning horse of mine, though.

262
00:22:39,859 --> 00:22:42,403
I owe that boy a big kiss when I see him.

263
00:22:43,029 --> 00:22:47,283
Why don't you give him some of your cigarettes instead? Lucky fuck!

264
00:22:47,533 --> 00:22:49,118
Hey, Tyrell.

265
00:22:49,452 --> 00:22:51,454
You pull infirmary duty this week?

266
00:22:52,371 --> 00:22:53,956
How's my horse doing anyway?

267
00:22:54,373 --> 00:22:55,500
Dead.

268
00:22:56,375 --> 00:22:58,544
Hadley busted his head up pretty good.

269
00:22:58,753 --> 00:23:00,963
Doc had gone home for the night.

270
00:23:01,464 --> 00:23:03,716
Poor bastard lay there till this morning.

271
00:23:03,925 --> 00:23:06,385
By then, there wasn't nothing we could do.

272
00:23:12,725 --> 00:23:14,018
What was his name?

273
00:23:17,395 --> 00:23:18,648
What'd you say?

274
00:23:20,565 --> 00:23:22,944
I was just wondering if anyone knew his name.

275
00:23:23,610 --> 00:23:26,364
What the fuck do you care, new fish?

276
00:23:27,406 --> 00:23:30,534
Doesn't fucking matter what his name was. He's dead.

277
00:23:52,598 --> 00:23:54,559
Anybody come at you yet?

278
00:23:56,727 --> 00:23:58,562
Anybody get to you yet?

279
00:24:00,231 --> 00:24:02,732
Hey, we all need friends in here.

280
00:24:03,067 --> 00:24:04,735
I could be a friend to you.

281
00:24:13,953 --> 00:24:15,328
Hard to get.

282
00:24:17,455 --> 00:24:18,915
I like that.

283
00:24:25,881 --> 00:24:29,093
Andy kept pretty much to himself at first.

284
00:24:29,467 --> 00:24:31,429
I guess he had a lot on his mind...

285
00:24:31,846 --> 00:24:34,681
...trying to adapt to life on the inside.

286
00:24:35,432 --> 00:24:38,602
Wasn't until a month went by before he opened his mouth...

287
00:24:38,811 --> 00:24:41,188
...to say more than two words to somebody.

288
00:24:42,398 --> 00:24:43,940
As it turned out...

289
00:24:44,442 --> 00:24:46,277
...that somebody was me.

290
00:24:55,076 --> 00:24:56,829
I'm Andy Dufresne.

291
00:24:58,079 --> 00:24:59,789
Wife-killing banker.

292
00:25:02,043 --> 00:25:03,376
Why'd you do it?

293
00:25:03,835 --> 00:25:05,879
I didn't, since you ask.

294
00:25:06,506 --> 00:25:08,298
You're going to fit right in.

295
00:25:08,549 --> 00:25:11,218
Everybody in here's innocent. Didn't you know that?

296
00:25:11,843 --> 00:25:15,056
- What you in here for? - Didn't do it. Lawyer fucked me.

297
00:25:18,099 --> 00:25:20,644
Rumor has it you're a real cold fish.

298
00:25:20,852 --> 00:25:24,481
You think your shit smells sweeter than most. Is that right?

299
00:25:24,689 --> 00:25:26,149
What do you think?

300
00:25:26,525 --> 00:25:29,569
To tell you the truth, I haven't made up my mind.

301
00:25:30,695 --> 00:25:33,490
I understand you're a man that knows how to get things.

302
00:25:34,491 --> 00:25:37,786
I'm known to locate certain things from time to time.

303
00:25:37,994 --> 00:25:40,539
I wonder if you might get me a rock hammer.

304
00:25:41,248 --> 00:25:42,541
A rock hammer.

305
00:25:42,917 --> 00:25:45,585
- What is it and why? - What do you care?

306
00:25:46,962 --> 00:25:49,673
For a toothbrush, I wouldn't ask. I'd quote a price.

307
00:25:49,881 --> 00:25:52,300
But a toothbrush is a non-lethal object, isn't it?

308
00:25:53,885 --> 00:25:55,262
Fair enough.

309
00:25:55,720 --> 00:25:58,348
A rock hammer is about six or seven inches long.

310
00:25:58,557 --> 00:26:00,851
- Looks like a miniature pickax. - Pickax?

311
00:26:01,059 --> 00:26:02,269
For rocks.

312
00:26:04,896 --> 00:26:06,106
Quartz?

313
00:26:10,067 --> 00:26:13,488
And some mica, shale...

314
00:26:14,739 --> 00:26:16,032
...limestone.

315
00:26:16,241 --> 00:26:17,367
So?

316
00:26:18,326 --> 00:26:20,162
So I'm a rock hound.

317
00:26:20,579 --> 00:26:23,707
At least I was in my old life. I'd like to be again.

318
00:26:23,957 --> 00:26:27,085
Or maybe you'd like to sink your toy into somebody's skull.

319
00:26:28,587 --> 00:26:30,130
No, I have no enemies here.

320
00:26:30,338 --> 00:26:32,632
No? Wait a while.

321
00:26:34,259 --> 00:26:35,844
Word gets around.

322
00:26:36,553 --> 00:26:39,014
The Sisters have taken quite a liking to you.

323
00:26:40,432 --> 00:26:42,225
Especially Bogs.

324
00:26:43,226 --> 00:26:46,646
Don't suppose it would help if I told them I'm not homosexual.

325
00:26:46,855 --> 00:26:48,273
Neither are they.

326
00:26:48,480 --> 00:26:51,942
You have to be human first. They don't qualify.

327
00:26:56,447 --> 00:27:00,869
Bull queers take by force. That's all they want or understand.

328
00:27:01,076 --> 00:27:04,581
If I were you, I'd grow eyes in the back of my head.

329
00:27:04,788 --> 00:27:07,583
- Thanks for the advice. - Well, that's free.

330
00:27:07,918 --> 00:27:09,877
You understand my concern.

331
00:27:10,086 --> 00:27:12,672
If there's trouble, I won't use the rock hammer.

332
00:27:13,590 --> 00:27:17,636
Then I'd guess you want to escape. Tunnel under the wall, maybe.

333
00:27:18,511 --> 00:27:20,222
What did I miss? What's so funny?

334
00:27:20,639 --> 00:27:23,307
You'll understand when you see the rock hammer.

335
00:27:25,143 --> 00:27:27,395
What's an item like this usually go for?

336
00:27:27,603 --> 00:27:30,064
Seven dollars in any rock-and-gem shop.

337
00:27:30,607 --> 00:27:33,068
My normal markup's 20 percent.

338
00:27:33,275 --> 00:27:35,611
But this is a specialty item.

339
00:27:35,821 --> 00:27:40,283
Risk goes up, price goes up. Let's make it an even 10 bucks.

340
00:27:40,491 --> 00:27:41,826
Ten it is.

341
00:27:43,578 --> 00:27:45,579
Waste of money, if you ask me.

342
00:27:45,871 --> 00:27:47,164
Why's that?

343
00:27:48,582 --> 00:27:51,585
Folks around this joint love surprise inspections.

344
00:27:53,212 --> 00:27:55,173
They find it, you're going to lose it.

345
00:27:55,381 --> 00:27:57,467
If they catch you, you don't know me.

346
00:27:57,675 --> 00:27:59,928
Mention my name, we never do business again.

347
00:28:00,427 --> 00:28:03,515
Not for shoelaces or a stick of gum. Now you got that?

348
00:28:04,348 --> 00:28:05,933
I understand.

349
00:28:07,143 --> 00:28:09,228
Thank you, Mr...

350
00:28:09,437 --> 00:28:10,437
Red.

351
00:28:11,189 --> 00:28:12,482
Name's Red.

352
00:28:13,942 --> 00:28:15,568
Why do they call you that?

353
00:28:19,363 --> 00:28:21,323
Maybe it's because I'm Irish.

354
00:28:29,541 --> 00:28:32,376
I could see why some of the boys took him for snobby.

355
00:28:33,252 --> 00:28:35,671
He had a quiet way about him...

356
00:28:36,046 --> 00:28:40,176
...a walk and a talk that just wasn't normal around here.

357
00:28:40,551 --> 00:28:42,011
He strolled...

358
00:28:42,219 --> 00:28:46,390
...like a man in the park without a care or a worry in the world.

359
00:28:46,599 --> 00:28:51,270
Like he had on an invisible coat that would shield him from this place.

360
00:28:53,230 --> 00:28:55,441
I think it'd be fair to say...

361
00:28:55,733 --> 00:28:57,943
...I liked Andy from the start.

362
00:28:59,487 --> 00:29:03,282
Let's go! Some of us got a schedule to keep.

363
00:29:06,619 --> 00:29:09,121
Move it! Come on, move it!

364
00:29:11,749 --> 00:29:14,668
How you doing? How's the wife treating you?

365
00:29:52,790 --> 00:29:54,250
Andy was right.

366
00:29:54,500 --> 00:29:56,751
I finally got the joke.

367
00:29:57,043 --> 00:29:59,421
It would take a man about 600 years...

368
00:29:59,630 --> 00:30:02,091
...to tunnel under the wall with one of these.

369
00:30:05,885 --> 00:30:08,304
- Book? - Not today.

370
00:30:14,353 --> 00:30:15,354
Book?

371
00:30:22,695 --> 00:30:24,864
Delivery for Dufresne.

372
00:30:42,547 --> 00:30:44,132
Here's your book.

373
00:30:49,179 --> 00:30:50,388
Thanks.

374
00:30:56,686 --> 00:31:00,356
We're running low on hexite. Get on back and fetch us up some.

375
00:31:37,685 --> 00:31:39,229
This will blind you.

376
00:31:39,604 --> 00:31:42,023
Honey, hush.

377
00:31:54,577 --> 00:31:56,538
That's it. You fight!

378
00:31:56,746 --> 00:31:58,206
Better that way.

379
00:32:06,422 --> 00:32:09,551
I wish I could tell you Andy fought the good fight...

380
00:32:09,759 --> 00:32:11,719
...and the Sisters let him be.

381
00:32:12,303 --> 00:32:14,138
I wish I could tell you that...

382
00:32:14,346 --> 00:32:17,308
...but prison is no fairy-tale world.

383
00:32:18,434 --> 00:32:20,436
He never said who did it.

384
00:32:20,645 --> 00:32:22,230
But we all knew.

385
00:32:27,110 --> 00:32:29,571
Things went on like that for a while.

386
00:32:29,988 --> 00:32:32,490
Prison life consists of routine...

387
00:32:32,782 --> 00:32:34,742
...and then more routine.

388
00:32:37,245 --> 00:32:40,748
Every so often, Andy would show up with fresh bruises.

389
00:32:42,249 --> 00:32:44,085
The Sisters kept at him.

390
00:32:44,294 --> 00:32:46,379
Sometimes he was able to fight them off.

391
00:32:46,587 --> 00:32:48,047
Sometimes not.

392
00:32:49,257 --> 00:32:51,426
And that's how it went for Andy.

393
00:32:51,968 --> 00:32:53,761
That was his routine.

394
00:32:54,596 --> 00:32:58,016
I do believe those first two years were the worst for him.

395
00:32:58,516 --> 00:33:01,894
And I also believe that if things had gone on that way...

396
00:33:02,228 --> 00:33:04,646
...this place would have got the best of him.

397
00:33:05,440 --> 00:33:08,233
But then, in the spring of 1949...

398
00:33:08,443 --> 00:33:10,320
...the powers that be decided:

399
00:33:10,527 --> 00:33:13,864
The roof of the license-plate factory needs resurfacing.

400
00:33:14,073 --> 00:33:17,285
I need a dozen volunteers for a week's work.

401
00:33:17,994 --> 00:33:19,454
As you know...

402
00:33:20,163 --> 00:33:23,750
...special detail carries with it special privileges.

403
00:33:23,957 --> 00:33:26,336
It was outdoor detail...

404
00:33:26,752 --> 00:33:31,007
...and May is one damned fine month to be working outdoors.

405
00:33:31,257 --> 00:33:32,592
Stay in line there.

406
00:33:32,799 --> 00:33:35,969
More than a hundred men volunteered for the job.

407
00:33:40,225 --> 00:33:43,227
Wallace E. Unger.

408
00:33:43,727 --> 00:33:46,104
Ellis Redding.

409
00:33:46,314 --> 00:33:47,857
Wouldn't you know it?

410
00:33:48,316 --> 00:33:51,610
Me and some fellows I know were among the names called.

411
00:33:51,819 --> 00:33:53,779
Andrew Dufresne.

412
00:33:54,196 --> 00:33:57,157
It only cost us a pack of smokes per man.

413
00:33:57,658 --> 00:34:00,118
I made my usual 20 percent, of course.

414
00:34:00,328 --> 00:34:03,790
So this big-shot lawyer calls me long-distance from Texas.

415
00:34:03,997 --> 00:34:05,541
I say, "Yeah?"

416
00:34:05,833 --> 00:34:09,460
He says, "Sorry to inform you, but your brother just died."

417
00:34:09,670 --> 00:34:13,549
- I'm sorry to hear that. - I'm not. He was an asshole.

418
00:34:13,757 --> 00:34:15,967
Ran off years ago. Figured him for dead.

419
00:34:16,176 --> 00:34:18,469
So this lawyer fellow says to me:

420
00:34:18,679 --> 00:34:22,808
"He died a rich man." Oil wells and shit. Close to a million bucks.

421
00:34:23,475 --> 00:34:25,351
A million bucks?

422
00:34:25,643 --> 00:34:30,149
- Incredible how lucky some assholes get. - You going to see any of that?

423
00:34:30,732 --> 00:34:33,527
Thirty-five thousand. That's what he left me.

424
00:34:33,735 --> 00:34:34,902
Dollars?

425
00:34:35,654 --> 00:34:38,657
That's great! That's like winning the sweepstakes.

426
00:34:38,991 --> 00:34:40,074
Isn't it?

427
00:34:40,284 --> 00:34:43,245
Dumb shit, what do you think the government will do to me?

428
00:34:43,453 --> 00:34:45,831
Take a big wet bite out of my ass is what.

429
00:34:46,581 --> 00:34:47,916
Poor Byron.

430
00:34:48,833 --> 00:34:52,004
Terrible fucking luck, huh? Crying shame.

431
00:34:52,212 --> 00:34:54,464
Some people really got it awful.

432
00:34:54,755 --> 00:34:56,091
Andy, are you nuts?

433
00:34:56,300 --> 00:34:58,218
Keep your eyes on your mop, man!

434
00:34:58,427 --> 00:35:01,388
You'll pay some tax, but you'll still end up-

435
00:35:01,597 --> 00:35:04,391
Yeah, maybe enough to buy a new car, and then what?

436
00:35:04,600 --> 00:35:06,727
I got to pay tax on the car. Repair...

437
00:35:06,977 --> 00:35:10,731
...maintenance, kids pestering you to take them for a ride all the time.

438
00:35:10,938 --> 00:35:14,610
Then if you figure your tax wrong, you pay out of your own pocket.

439
00:35:14,818 --> 00:35:16,945
I tell you! Uncle Sam!

440
00:35:17,196 --> 00:35:21,199
He puts his hand in your shirt and squeezes your tit till it's purple.

441
00:35:23,827 --> 00:35:25,996
- Getting himself killed. - Keep tarring.

442
00:35:26,245 --> 00:35:28,749
Some brother. Shit!

443
00:35:33,712 --> 00:35:35,088
Mr. Hadley...

444
00:35:35,964 --> 00:35:37,341
...do you trust your wife?

445
00:35:38,966 --> 00:35:40,427
Oh, that's funny.

446
00:35:40,968 --> 00:35:43,596
You'll look funnier sucking my dick with no teeth.

447
00:35:43,805 --> 00:35:47,142
What I mean is, do you think she'd go behind your back?

448
00:35:48,310 --> 00:35:51,647
Step aside, Mert. This fucker's having himself an accident.

449
00:35:51,897 --> 00:35:52,898
He'll push him off!

450
00:35:53,148 --> 00:35:54,982
If you trust her, you can keep...

451
00:35:55,234 --> 00:35:56,610
...that 35,000.

452
00:35:57,361 --> 00:35:59,863
- What did you say? - Thirty-five thousand.

453
00:36:00,697 --> 00:36:01,740
All of it.

454
00:36:01,948 --> 00:36:04,993
- Every penny. - You better start making sense.

455
00:36:05,202 --> 00:36:07,537
If you want to keep it, give it to your wife.

456
00:36:07,746 --> 00:36:11,624
The IRS allows a one-time-only gift to your spouse for up to $60,000.

457
00:36:11,834 --> 00:36:14,210
- Bullshit. Tax-free? - Tax-free.

458
00:36:14,419 --> 00:36:16,379
IRS can't touch one cent.

459
00:36:16,796 --> 00:36:19,257
You're that smart banker that killed his wife.

460
00:36:19,508 --> 00:36:22,844
Why should I believe you? So I can end up in here with you?

461
00:36:23,053 --> 00:36:25,888
It's legal. Ask the IRS. They'll say the same thing.

462
00:36:26,098 --> 00:36:30,144
I feel stupid telling you this. I'm sure you would have investigated.

463
00:36:30,351 --> 00:36:34,021
I don't need you to tell me where the bear shit in the buckwheat.

464
00:36:34,231 --> 00:36:37,651
Of course not. But you do need someone to set it up for you.

465
00:36:37,859 --> 00:36:39,403
That'll cost you. A lawyer.

466
00:36:39,610 --> 00:36:41,864
A bunch of ball-washing bastards!

467
00:36:42,322 --> 00:36:45,784
I suppose I could set it up for you. That would save you some money.

468
00:36:46,034 --> 00:36:48,078
You get the forms, I'll prepare them...

469
00:36:48,327 --> 00:36:50,037
...nearly free of charge.

470
00:36:51,289 --> 00:36:54,585
I'd only ask three beers apiece for each of my coworkers.

471
00:36:55,543 --> 00:36:57,086
"Coworkers." That's rich!

472
00:36:57,920 --> 00:37:00,464
A man working outdoors feels more like a man...

473
00:37:00,673 --> 00:37:04,135
...if he can have a bottle of suds. That's only my opinion...

474
00:37:04,553 --> 00:37:05,554
...sir.

475
00:37:09,515 --> 00:37:11,434
What are you jimmies staring at?

476
00:37:11,642 --> 00:37:13,227
Let's go! Work!

477
00:37:22,738 --> 00:37:24,697
And that's how it came to pass...

478
00:37:24,907 --> 00:37:27,533
...that on the second-to-last day of the job...

479
00:37:27,742 --> 00:37:32,330
...the convict crew that tarred the factory roof in the spring of '49...

480
00:37:32,538 --> 00:37:35,666
...wound up sitting in a row at 10:00 in the morning...

481
00:37:35,875 --> 00:37:41,339
...drinking icy-cold beer, courtesy of the hardest screw...

482
00:37:41,547 --> 00:37:44,800
...that ever walked a turn at Shawshank State Prison.

483
00:37:45,009 --> 00:37:47,428
Drink up while it's cold, ladies.

484
00:37:48,554 --> 00:37:53,100
The colossal prick even managed to sound magnanimous.

485
00:37:54,268 --> 00:37:58,689
We sat and drank with the sun on our shoulders and felt like free men.

486
00:37:59,106 --> 00:38:02,527
We could have been tarring the roof of one of our own houses.

487
00:38:02,735 --> 00:38:05,571
We were the lords of all creation.

488
00:38:06,113 --> 00:38:07,698
As for Andy...

489
00:38:07,948 --> 00:38:10,576
...he spent that break hunkered in the shade...

490
00:38:10,785 --> 00:38:13,286
...a strange little smile on his face...

491
00:38:13,538 --> 00:38:15,747
...watching us drink his beer.

492
00:38:23,839 --> 00:38:25,216
Want a cold one?

493
00:38:25,883 --> 00:38:28,803
No, thanks. I gave up drinking.

494
00:38:32,806 --> 00:38:35,852
You could argue he done it to curry favor with the guards.

495
00:38:36,560 --> 00:38:40,356
Or maybe make a few friends among us cons.

496
00:38:41,107 --> 00:38:42,150
Me?

497
00:38:42,358 --> 00:38:45,153
I think he did it just to feel normal again...

498
00:38:45,611 --> 00:38:47,821
...if only for a short while.

499
00:38:50,449 --> 00:38:51,575
King me.

500
00:38:53,451 --> 00:38:56,080
- Chess. Now there's a game of kings. - What?

501
00:38:56,289 --> 00:38:57,999
Civilized. Strategic.

502
00:38:58,665 --> 00:39:01,668
And a total fucking mystery. I hate it.

503
00:39:01,960 --> 00:39:03,712
Let me teach you someday.

504
00:39:05,297 --> 00:39:06,257
Sure.

505
00:39:06,715 --> 00:39:08,466
We could get a board together.

506
00:39:08,676 --> 00:39:11,761
You're talking to the right man. I can get things, right?

507
00:39:11,970 --> 00:39:15,933
We might do business on a board, and I'll carve the pieces myself.

508
00:39:16,184 --> 00:39:19,478
One side in alabaster, one in soapstone. What do you think?

509
00:39:20,062 --> 00:39:21,480
I think it'll take years.

510
00:39:21,646 --> 00:39:24,191
Years I got. What I don't have are the rocks.

511
00:39:24,400 --> 00:39:26,359
Pickings are pretty slim in the yard.

512
00:39:26,569 --> 00:39:28,403
Pebbles, mostly.

513
00:39:32,741 --> 00:39:35,536
We're getting to be kind of friends, aren't we?

514
00:39:36,412 --> 00:39:37,954
Yeah, I guess.

515
00:39:38,622 --> 00:39:40,123
Can I ask you something?

516
00:39:41,626 --> 00:39:43,044
Why'd you do it?

517
00:39:44,294 --> 00:39:45,837
I'm innocent, Red.

518
00:39:46,046 --> 00:39:48,089
Just like everybody else here.

519
00:39:52,427 --> 00:39:53,970
What are you in for?

520
00:39:56,681 --> 00:39:58,850
Murder. Same as you.

521
00:39:59,059 --> 00:40:00,227
Innocent?

522
00:40:02,938 --> 00:40:05,357
Only guilty man in Shawshank.

523
00:41:04,666 --> 00:41:06,168
Where's the canary?

524
00:41:06,376 --> 00:41:07,669
How did you know?

525
00:41:07,878 --> 00:41:10,171
- How did I know what? - So you don't know.

526
00:41:10,380 --> 00:41:11,548
Come.

527
00:41:14,050 --> 00:41:16,136
This is where the canary is.

528
00:41:18,805 --> 00:41:22,141
Quite a surprise to hear a woman singing in my house, eh?

529
00:41:25,645 --> 00:41:27,062
That's quite a...

530
00:41:27,981 --> 00:41:29,648
...surprise.

531
00:41:33,945 --> 00:41:35,363
Wait, wait.

532
00:41:35,906 --> 00:41:37,407
Here she comes.

533
00:41:37,657 --> 00:41:40,535
I like this part when she does that shit with her hair.

534
00:41:40,744 --> 00:41:43,455
I know. I've seen it three times this month.

535
00:41:43,662 --> 00:41:45,582
Gilda, are you decent?

536
00:41:46,582 --> 00:41:47,708
Me?

537
00:41:50,420 --> 00:41:52,172
God, I love it.

538
00:41:57,344 --> 00:42:00,347
I understand you're a man that knows how to get things.

539
00:42:01,348 --> 00:42:05,101
I'm known to locate certain things from time to time. What do you want?

540
00:42:05,310 --> 00:42:07,437
- Rita Hayworth. - What?

541
00:42:07,979 --> 00:42:09,356
Can you get her?

542
00:42:10,774 --> 00:42:14,151
So this is Johnny Farrel. I've heard a lot about you.

543
00:42:15,362 --> 00:42:17,656
- Take a few weeks. - Weeks?

544
00:42:17,864 --> 00:42:22,285
I don't have her stuffed down the front of my pants right now, sorry to say.

545
00:42:22,827 --> 00:42:24,162
But I'll get her.

546
00:42:24,371 --> 00:42:25,704
Relax.

547
00:42:27,581 --> 00:42:28,874
Thanks.

548
00:42:49,479 --> 00:42:51,230
- Get out! - I got to change reels!

549
00:42:51,481 --> 00:42:53,482
I said fuck off!

550
00:42:59,613 --> 00:43:00,990
Ain't you going to scream?

551
00:43:02,325 --> 00:43:04,202
Let's get this over with.

552
00:43:09,290 --> 00:43:11,293
He broke my fucking nose!

553
00:43:20,593 --> 00:43:21,844
Now...

554
00:43:22,303 --> 00:43:24,472
...I'm going to open my fly...

555
00:43:24,723 --> 00:43:27,516
...and you'll swallow what I give you to swallow.

556
00:43:28,642 --> 00:43:31,228
Then you'll swallow Rooster's. You broke his nose.

557
00:43:31,437 --> 00:43:33,564
He ought to have something to show for it.

558
00:43:33,772 --> 00:43:35,649
You put it in my mouth, you lose it.

559
00:43:35,858 --> 00:43:38,444
No, you don't understand.

560
00:43:38,777 --> 00:43:41,822
Do that and I'll put all eight inches of this in your ear.

561
00:43:42,031 --> 00:43:45,493
All right, but you should know that sudden, serious brain injury...

562
00:43:45,701 --> 00:43:47,620
...causes the victim to bite down hard.

563
00:43:47,828 --> 00:43:51,332
In fact, I hear the bite reflex is so strong...

564
00:43:52,124 --> 00:43:54,585
...they have to pry the victim's jaws open...

565
00:43:54,793 --> 00:43:56,170
...with a crowbar.

566
00:43:59,131 --> 00:44:00,673
Where do you get this shit?

567
00:44:00,883 --> 00:44:02,258
I read it.

568
00:44:03,677 --> 00:44:06,347
You know how to read, you ignorant fuck?

569
00:44:08,015 --> 00:44:09,099
Honey!

570
00:44:11,060 --> 00:44:12,770
You shouldn't!

571
00:44:15,481 --> 00:44:18,359
Bogs didn't put anything in Andy's mouth.

572
00:44:18,651 --> 00:44:20,694
And neither did his friends.

573
00:44:21,111 --> 00:44:24,948
What they did do is beat him within an inch of his life.

574
00:44:25,657 --> 00:44:28,117
Andy spent a month in the infirmary.

575
00:44:28,953 --> 00:44:31,454
Bogs spent a week in the hole.

576
00:44:35,543 --> 00:44:37,085
Time's up, Bogs.

577
00:44:40,881 --> 00:44:42,549
It's your world, boss.

578
00:44:45,803 --> 00:44:48,638
Return to your cellblocks for evening count.

579
00:44:48,806 --> 00:44:51,475
All prisoners report for lock down.

580
00:45:06,406 --> 00:45:07,449
What?

581
00:45:20,086 --> 00:45:22,631
- Where's he going? - Grab his ankles.

582
00:45:28,429 --> 00:45:29,679
Help!

583
00:45:33,350 --> 00:45:35,977
Two things never happened again after that.

584
00:45:36,228 --> 00:45:39,355
The Sisters never laid a finger on Andy again.

585
00:45:40,190 --> 00:45:42,692
And Bogs would never walk again.

586
00:45:43,861 --> 00:45:47,364
They transferred him to a minimum-security hospital upstate.

587
00:45:47,572 --> 00:45:50,367
To my knowledge, he lived out the rest of his days...

588
00:45:50,575 --> 00:45:52,368
...drinking his food through a straw.

589
00:45:53,369 --> 00:45:57,499
Andy could use a nice welcome back when he gets out of the infirmary.

590
00:45:59,210 --> 00:46:00,794
Sounds good to us.

591
00:46:01,044 --> 00:46:03,546
I figure we owe him that much for the beer.

592
00:46:04,297 --> 00:46:06,550
The man likes to play chess.

593
00:46:07,050 --> 00:46:08,968
Let's get him some rocks.

594
00:46:41,626 --> 00:46:42,836
Guys!

595
00:46:43,628 --> 00:46:44,671
I got one.

596
00:46:44,879 --> 00:46:46,381
I got one. Look!

597
00:46:48,258 --> 00:46:52,344
Heywood, that isn't soapstone! And it ain't alabaster either.

598
00:46:52,554 --> 00:46:54,430
What are you, a fucking geologist?

599
00:46:54,639 --> 00:46:56,390
He's right. It ain't.

600
00:46:56,599 --> 00:46:59,477
- What the hell is it then? - It's a horse apple.

601
00:47:00,435 --> 00:47:02,856
- Bullshit! - No, horseshit.

602
00:47:03,064 --> 00:47:04,524
Petrified.

603
00:47:13,533 --> 00:47:14,741
Damn!

604
00:47:15,034 --> 00:47:19,289
Despite a few hitches, the boys came through in fine style.

605
00:47:19,455 --> 00:47:21,457
And by the weekend he was due back...

606
00:47:21,665 --> 00:47:25,378
...we had enough rocks saved up to keep him busy till rapture.

607
00:47:26,212 --> 00:47:28,715
Also got a big shipment in that week.

608
00:47:28,965 --> 00:47:30,091
Cigarettes...

609
00:47:30,300 --> 00:47:31,341
...chewing gum...

610
00:47:31,551 --> 00:47:33,011
...sipping whisky...

611
00:47:33,218 --> 00:47:36,639
...playing cards with naked ladies on them. You name it.

612
00:47:36,847 --> 00:47:39,559
And of course, the most important item:

613
00:47:41,436 --> 00:47:43,479
Rita Hayworth herself.

614
00:48:20,599 --> 00:48:22,852
Heads up. They're tossing cells.

615
00:48:23,060 --> 00:48:25,771
Heads up. They're tossing cells!

616
00:48:27,356 --> 00:48:28,857
119.

617
00:48:30,692 --> 00:48:32,362
123.

618
00:48:42,830 --> 00:48:43,665
On your feet.

619
00:48:46,291 --> 00:48:47,793
Face the wall.

620
00:49:21,952 --> 00:49:23,829
Turn around and face the warden.

621
00:49:30,419 --> 00:49:32,504
Pleased to see you reading this.

622
00:49:33,839 --> 00:49:35,757
Any favorite passages?

623
00:49:36,633 --> 00:49:39,011
"Watch ye, therefore, for ye know not...

624
00:49:39,219 --> 00:49:41,430
...when the master of the house cometh."

625
00:49:42,598 --> 00:49:44,766
Mark 13:35.

626
00:49:45,476 --> 00:49:47,352
I've always liked that one.

627
00:49:48,270 --> 00:49:49,939
But I prefer...

628
00:49:50,439 --> 00:49:52,274
..."I am the light of the world.

629
00:49:52,774 --> 00:49:56,069
Ye that followeth me shall have the light of life."

630
00:49:56,278 --> 00:49:58,906
John, chapter 8, verse 12.

631
00:49:59,156 --> 00:50:01,200
I hear you're good with numbers.

632
00:50:01,408 --> 00:50:02,409
How nice.

633
00:50:03,619 --> 00:50:05,579
Man should have a skill.

634
00:50:08,123 --> 00:50:09,124
Explain this.

635
00:50:10,292 --> 00:50:14,379
It's called a rock blanket. It's for shaping and polishing rocks.

636
00:50:14,796 --> 00:50:16,715
A little hobby of mine.

637
00:50:27,601 --> 00:50:29,019
It's pretty clean.

638
00:50:29,311 --> 00:50:32,396
Some contraband here, but nothing to get in a twist over.

639
00:50:36,734 --> 00:50:38,737
I can't say I approve of this.

640
00:50:40,363 --> 00:50:41,824
But I suppose...

641
00:50:43,909 --> 00:50:45,911
...exceptions can be made.

642
00:50:56,296 --> 00:50:57,631
Lock them up!

643
00:51:00,509 --> 00:51:02,094
I almost forgot.

644
00:51:02,511 --> 00:51:04,680
I'd hate to deprive you of this.

645
00:51:05,430 --> 00:51:07,348
Salvation lies within.

646
00:51:07,558 --> 00:51:08,641
Yes, sir.

647
00:51:17,191 --> 00:51:19,736
Tossing cells was just an excuse.

648
00:51:20,194 --> 00:51:21,446
Truth is...

649
00:51:21,654 --> 00:51:24,115
...Norton wanted to size Andy up.

650
00:51:37,795 --> 00:51:40,131
My wife made that in church group.

651
00:51:42,801 --> 00:51:44,094
Very nice, sir.

652
00:51:45,678 --> 00:51:47,139
You enjoy working the laundry?

653
00:51:48,806 --> 00:51:50,516
No, sir. Not especially.

654
00:51:50,850 --> 00:51:53,269
Perhaps we can find something more...

655
00:51:53,561 --> 00:51:55,981
...befitting a man of your education.

656
00:52:04,071 --> 00:52:05,531
Hey, Jake. Where's Brooks?

657
00:52:07,408 --> 00:52:10,161
I thought I heard you out here.

658
00:52:10,829 --> 00:52:12,371
I've been reassigned to you.

659
00:52:12,663 --> 00:52:14,999
I know, they told me.

660
00:52:15,833 --> 00:52:17,877
Ain't that a kick in the head?

661
00:52:18,085 --> 00:52:20,421
Well, I'll give you the dime tour.

662
00:52:20,588 --> 00:52:21,756
Come on.

663
00:52:23,174 --> 00:52:24,926
Well, here she is.

664
00:52:25,092 --> 00:52:27,094
The Shawshank Prison Library.

665
00:52:27,762 --> 00:52:29,765
National Geographics...

666
00:52:30,681 --> 00:52:33,476
...Reader's Digest condensed books...

667
00:52:33,684 --> 00:52:35,269
...and Louis L'Amour.

668
00:52:35,853 --> 00:52:37,688
Look magazine.

669
00:52:38,022 --> 00:52:40,358
Erle Stanley Gardners.

670
00:52:40,608 --> 00:52:44,070
Every evening I load up the cart and make my rounds.

671
00:52:44,278 --> 00:52:46,948
I enter the names on this clipboard here.

672
00:52:47,448 --> 00:52:50,201
Easy, peasy, Japanese-y.

673
00:52:50,952 --> 00:52:53,830
- Any questions? - How long have you been librarian?

674
00:52:55,038 --> 00:52:59,084
I come here in '05, and they made me librarian in 1912.

675
00:53:00,378 --> 00:53:02,546
And have you ever had an assistant?

676
00:53:02,797 --> 00:53:05,216
No. Not much to it, really.

677
00:53:05,967 --> 00:53:07,218
Why me? Why now?

678
00:53:07,635 --> 00:53:08,970
I don't know.

679
00:53:09,137 --> 00:53:12,181
But it'd be nice to have some company down here.

680
00:53:23,442 --> 00:53:25,736
That's him. That's the one.

681
00:53:33,369 --> 00:53:34,537
I'm Dekins.

682
00:53:34,954 --> 00:53:36,581
I was thinking...

683
00:53:37,248 --> 00:53:41,126
...about setting up some kind of trust fund for my kids' educations.

684
00:53:45,005 --> 00:53:46,507
I see.

685
00:53:51,596 --> 00:53:54,556
Why don't we have a seat and talk it over.

686
00:53:57,768 --> 00:54:00,730
Do you have a piece of paper and a pencil?

687
00:54:12,074 --> 00:54:13,367
Thanks.

688
00:54:14,034 --> 00:54:15,119
So...

689
00:54:16,871 --> 00:54:18,623
Mister Dekins...

690
00:54:19,457 --> 00:54:21,541
And then Andy says, "Mr. Dekins...

691
00:54:21,751 --> 00:54:24,753
...do you want your sons to go to Harvard or Yale?"

692
00:54:24,962 --> 00:54:26,004
He didn't say that!

693
00:54:26,380 --> 00:54:28,841
As God as my witness!

694
00:54:29,133 --> 00:54:30,885
Dekins blinked for a second...

695
00:54:31,052 --> 00:54:34,180
...then he laughed and actually shook Andy's hand.

696
00:54:34,639 --> 00:54:36,890
- My ass! - Shook his hand.

697
00:54:37,140 --> 00:54:40,060
I tell you, I near soiled myself!

698
00:54:40,227 --> 00:54:44,523
All he needed was a suit and tie and a jiggly hula gal on his desk...

699
00:54:44,732 --> 00:54:47,317
...he'd have been "Mr. Dufresne," if you please.

700
00:54:47,526 --> 00:54:49,152
Making a few friends?

701
00:54:50,445 --> 00:54:52,114
I wouldn't say "friends."

702
00:54:52,322 --> 00:54:56,160
I'm a convicted murderer who provides sound financial planning.

703
00:54:56,368 --> 00:54:58,703
It's a wonderful pet to have.

704
00:54:58,913 --> 00:55:00,705
Got you out of the laundry, though.

705
00:55:01,248 --> 00:55:03,124
It might do more than that.

706
00:55:03,376 --> 00:55:06,086
How about expanding the library. Get some new books.

707
00:55:06,254 --> 00:55:08,881
If you ask for something, ask for a pool table.

708
00:55:10,591 --> 00:55:13,552
How do you expect to do that? I mean...

709
00:55:13,760 --> 00:55:16,763
...get new books in here, "Mr. Dufresne, if you please."

710
00:55:17,222 --> 00:55:19,267
I'll ask the warden for funds.

711
00:55:20,143 --> 00:55:24,437
Six wardens have been through here in my tenure, and I've learned...

712
00:55:24,646 --> 00:55:26,940
...one immutable, universal truth:

713
00:55:27,190 --> 00:55:28,608
Not one born whose asshole...

714
00:55:28,775 --> 00:55:33,738
...wouldn't pucker up tighter than a snare drum when you ask for funds.

715
00:55:34,614 --> 00:55:37,492
- The budget's stretched thin as it is. - I see.

716
00:55:37,701 --> 00:55:41,121
Maybe I could write the state senate and request funds from them.

717
00:55:41,329 --> 00:55:45,417
They have only three ways to spend the taxpayers' money for prisons:

718
00:55:45,625 --> 00:55:48,086
More walls, more bars, more guards.

719
00:55:48,295 --> 00:55:50,922
I'd like to try, with permission. A letter a week.

720
00:55:51,131 --> 00:55:53,884
- They can't ignore me forever. - Sure can.

721
00:55:54,134 --> 00:55:56,803
But you write your letters if it makes you happy.

722
00:55:57,137 --> 00:55:59,723
I'll even mail them for you. How's that?

723
00:56:01,391 --> 00:56:04,185
So Andy started writing a letter a week...

724
00:56:04,394 --> 00:56:05,979
...just like he said.

725
00:56:10,150 --> 00:56:12,777
And like Norton said...

726
00:56:12,986 --> 00:56:14,488
...Andy got no answers.

727
00:56:22,661 --> 00:56:27,583
The following April, he did tax returns for half the guards at Shawshank.

728
00:56:28,919 --> 00:56:31,171
Year after that, he did them all...

729
00:56:31,421 --> 00:56:33,340
...including the warden's.

730
00:56:35,090 --> 00:56:38,552
Year after that, they rescheduled the intramural season...

731
00:56:38,762 --> 00:56:41,180
...to coincide with tax season.

732
00:56:42,765 --> 00:56:47,228
The guards on the opposing teams all remembered to bring their W-2s.

733
00:56:47,437 --> 00:56:48,772
So Moresby Prison...

734
00:56:48,979 --> 00:56:51,233
...issued you a gun, but you paid for it.

735
00:56:51,483 --> 00:56:53,193
Right. The holster too.

736
00:56:53,360 --> 00:56:55,862
That's tax-deductible. You can write that off.

737
00:56:56,028 --> 00:56:59,073
Yes, sir! Andy was a regular cottage industry.

738
00:56:59,782 --> 00:57:04,286
In fact, it got so busy at tax time, he was allowed a staff.

739
00:57:04,537 --> 00:57:07,039
Could you hand me a stack of 1040s?

740
00:57:07,207 --> 00:57:12,044
Got me out of the wood shop a month out of the year, and that was fine by me.

741
00:57:13,296 --> 00:57:16,466
And still, he kept sending those letters.

742
00:57:23,222 --> 00:57:24,266
It's Brooks.

743
00:57:25,391 --> 00:57:26,893
Watch the door.

744
00:57:27,144 --> 00:57:28,478
Please, Brooks.

745
00:57:28,728 --> 00:57:30,563
- Calm the fuck down. - Stay back!

746
00:57:30,980 --> 00:57:33,607
- Stay back, goddamn it! - What's going on?

747
00:57:33,817 --> 00:57:36,360
One second he's fine, then out come the knives.

748
00:57:36,570 --> 00:57:38,404
We can talk about this, right?

749
00:57:38,654 --> 00:57:42,408
There's nothing to talk about. I'll cut his fucking throat.

750
00:57:42,658 --> 00:57:44,410
What's he done to you?

751
00:57:44,577 --> 00:57:46,246
It's what they done!

752
00:57:46,745 --> 00:57:48,038
I got no choice.

753
00:57:48,497 --> 00:57:50,791
You won't hurt Heywood. We all know that.

754
00:57:51,001 --> 00:57:53,462
- Right, Heywood? - Sure.

755
00:57:53,711 --> 00:57:57,048
He's a friend of yours, and Brooks is a reasonable man.

756
00:57:57,298 --> 00:57:58,383
Right, guys?

757
00:57:59,341 --> 00:58:01,886
So put the knife down. Look at me.

758
00:58:02,094 --> 00:58:04,346
Put the knife down.

759
00:58:06,265 --> 00:58:08,392
Look at his neck, for God's sake.

760
00:58:09,101 --> 00:58:11,437
Look at his neck. He's bleeding.

761
00:58:12,354 --> 00:58:13,815
It's the only way...

762
00:58:14,023 --> 00:58:16,066
...they'd let me stay.

763
00:58:16,358 --> 00:58:18,737
This is crazy. You don't want to do this.

764
00:58:18,944 --> 00:58:21,030
Put it, put it down.

765
00:58:29,788 --> 00:58:31,290
Take it easy.

766
00:58:31,957 --> 00:58:33,542
You'll be all right.

767
00:58:34,585 --> 00:58:36,170
Him? What about me?

768
00:58:36,378 --> 00:58:38,798
Crazy old fool damn near cut my throat!

769
00:58:38,964 --> 00:58:40,883
You've had worse from shaving.

770
00:58:41,091 --> 00:58:43,177
What did you do to set him off?

771
00:58:43,385 --> 00:58:45,638
Nothing. I come in here to say farewell.

772
00:58:46,222 --> 00:58:48,891
Ain't you heard? His parole's come through.

773
00:58:51,227 --> 00:58:54,021
I just don't understand what happened in there.

774
00:58:54,230 --> 00:58:57,024
Old man's crazy as a rat in a tin shithouse.

775
00:58:57,233 --> 00:58:59,151
That's enough out of you.

776
00:58:59,317 --> 00:59:02,070
- Heard he had you shitting your pants. - Fuck you.

777
00:59:02,321 --> 00:59:03,531
Knock it off.

778
00:59:04,490 --> 00:59:05,950
Brooks ain't no bug.

779
00:59:09,787 --> 00:59:11,539
He's just institutionalized.

780
00:59:12,915 --> 00:59:14,834
"Institutionalized," my ass.

781
00:59:15,042 --> 00:59:19,046
The man's been in here 50 years, Heywood, 50 years!

782
00:59:19,254 --> 00:59:21,006
This is all he knows.

783
00:59:21,173 --> 00:59:23,509
In here, he's an important man...

784
00:59:23,676 --> 00:59:24,802
...an educated man.

785
00:59:25,593 --> 00:59:27,513
Outside, he's nothing.

786
00:59:28,389 --> 00:59:31,892
Just a used-up con with arthritis in both hands.

787
00:59:32,100 --> 00:59:35,104
Probably couldn't get a library card if he tried.

788
00:59:35,354 --> 00:59:37,106
You know what I'm trying to say?

789
00:59:37,356 --> 00:59:40,276
I do believe you're talking out of your ass.

790
00:59:41,527 --> 00:59:43,779
You believe whatever you want.

791
00:59:44,363 --> 00:59:46,782
But I tell you these walls are funny.

792
00:59:48,367 --> 00:59:50,034
First you hate them.

793
00:59:51,871 --> 00:59:53,788
Then you get used to them.

794
00:59:55,708 --> 00:59:57,710
Enough time passes...

795
00:59:58,376 --> 01:00:00,379
...you get so you depend on them.

796
01:00:01,380 --> 01:00:03,590
That's "institutionalized."

797
01:00:03,799 --> 01:00:05,092
Shit.

798
01:00:05,301 --> 01:00:08,052
- I could never get like that. - Oh, yeah?

799
01:00:08,928 --> 01:00:11,431
Wait till you've been here as long as Brooks.

800
01:00:11,681 --> 01:00:13,225
Goddamn right.

801
01:00:15,811 --> 01:00:17,729
They send you here for life...

802
01:00:18,397 --> 01:00:20,733
...that's exactly what they take.

803
01:00:22,485 --> 01:00:24,487
Part that counts, anyway.

804
01:00:32,076 --> 01:00:35,039
I can't take care of you no more, Jake.

805
01:00:36,206 --> 01:00:37,874
You go on now.

806
01:00:39,084 --> 01:00:40,585
You're free.

807
01:00:41,878 --> 01:00:43,421
You're free.

808
01:01:02,440 --> 01:01:04,108
Good luck, Brooksie.

809
01:01:42,105 --> 01:01:43,606
Dear fellas:

810
01:01:43,857 --> 01:01:48,069
I can't believe how fast things move on the outside.

811
01:01:49,362 --> 01:01:52,031
Watch it, old-timer! Want to get killed?

812
01:01:53,283 --> 01:01:56,786
I saw an automobile once when I was a kid...

813
01:01:57,412 --> 01:01:59,831
...but now they're everywhere.

814
01:02:03,501 --> 01:02:07,839
The world went and got itself in a big damn hurry.

815
01:02:23,645 --> 01:02:27,399
The parole board got me into this halfway house...

816
01:02:27,691 --> 01:02:29,360
...called "The Brewer"...

817
01:02:29,568 --> 01:02:30,737
...and a job...

818
01:02:30,945 --> 01:02:33,906
...bagging groceries at the Food-Way.

819
01:02:35,450 --> 01:02:37,869
It's hard work and I try to keep up...

820
01:02:38,076 --> 01:02:40,455
...but my hands hurt most of the time.

821
01:02:40,747 --> 01:02:42,706
Make sure your man double-bags.

822
01:02:42,916 --> 01:02:44,959
Last time, the bottom near came out.

823
01:02:45,627 --> 01:02:48,546
Make sure you double-bag like the lady says. Understand?

824
01:02:48,754 --> 01:02:50,131
Yes, sir. Surely will.

825
01:02:50,339 --> 01:02:53,842
I don't think the store manager likes me very much.

826
01:02:56,888 --> 01:03:01,183
Sometimes after work, I go to the park and feed the birds.

827
01:03:01,600 --> 01:03:03,102
I keep thinking...

828
01:03:03,352 --> 01:03:06,688
...Jake might just show up and say hello.

829
01:03:07,272 --> 01:03:09,274
But he never does.

830
01:03:10,485 --> 01:03:15,364
I hope, wherever he is, he's doing okay and making new friends.

831
01:03:18,951 --> 01:03:21,703
I have trouble sleeping at night.

832
01:03:21,995 --> 01:03:25,250
I have bad dreams like I'm falling.

833
01:03:25,542 --> 01:03:27,751
I wake up scared.

834
01:03:27,961 --> 01:03:32,214
Sometimes it takes me a while to remember where I am.

835
01:03:33,715 --> 01:03:38,595
Maybe I should get a gun and rob the Food-Way so they'd send me home.

836
01:03:38,929 --> 01:03:43,267
I could shoot the manager while I was at it. Sort of like a bonus.

837
01:03:44,935 --> 01:03:48,897
I guess I'm too old for that sort of nonsense anymore.

838
01:03:49,148 --> 01:03:50,899
I don't like it here.

839
01:03:51,109 --> 01:03:55,070
I'm tired of being afraid all the time. I've decided...

840
01:03:55,946 --> 01:03:57,573
...not to stay.

841
01:04:08,500 --> 01:04:11,086
I doubt they'll kick up any fuss...

842
01:04:11,295 --> 01:04:14,756
...not for an old crook like me.

843
01:05:17,236 --> 01:05:21,531
"I doubt they'll kick up any fuss, not for an old crook like me.

844
01:05:22,532 --> 01:05:26,370
P.S. Tell Heywood I'm sorry I put a knife to his throat.

845
01:05:26,577 --> 01:05:28,914
No hard feelings. Brooks."

846
01:05:39,383 --> 01:05:41,385
He should have died in here.

847
01:05:46,890 --> 01:05:48,308
What the fuck have you done?

848
01:05:48,516 --> 01:05:51,186
It's a goddamn mess, I'll tell you that.

849
01:05:56,732 --> 01:06:00,445
- What's all this? - You tell me. They're addressed to you.

850
01:06:01,738 --> 01:06:03,073
Take it.

851
01:06:11,582 --> 01:06:13,082
"Dear Mr. Dufresne:

852
01:06:14,250 --> 01:06:16,211
In response to your inquiries...

853
01:06:16,419 --> 01:06:20,674
...the state has allocated the enclosed funds for your library project."

854
01:06:21,424 --> 01:06:23,260
This is $200.

855
01:06:23,468 --> 01:06:27,473
"In addition, the library district has generously responded...

856
01:06:27,680 --> 01:06:30,225
...with a donation of used books and sundries.

857
01:06:30,433 --> 01:06:34,562
We trust this will fill your needs. We now consider the matter closed.

858
01:06:34,770 --> 01:06:36,606
Please stop sending us letters."

859
01:06:36,814 --> 01:06:39,484
Clear all this out before the warden gets back.

860
01:06:39,735 --> 01:06:40,903
Yes, sir.

861
01:06:45,156 --> 01:06:46,324
Good for you, Andy.

862
01:06:49,286 --> 01:06:50,412
Wow!

863
01:06:51,621 --> 01:06:53,582
It only took six years.

864
01:06:55,124 --> 01:06:57,877
From now on, I'll write two letters a week instead of one.

865
01:06:58,086 --> 01:07:01,422
I believe you're crazy enough. Get this stuff out...

866
01:07:01,673 --> 01:07:02,507
...like he said.

867
01:07:02,716 --> 01:07:04,134
I've got to pinch a loaf.

868
01:07:04,301 --> 01:07:05,802
When I come back...

869
01:07:05,968 --> 01:07:07,763
...this is all gone, all right?

870
01:07:56,519 --> 01:07:57,895
Do you hear that?

871
01:09:06,630 --> 01:09:07,798
Dufresne!

872
01:09:10,343 --> 01:09:11,886
Andy, let me out!

873
01:09:18,767 --> 01:09:23,688
I have no idea to this day what those two Italian ladies were singing about.

874
01:09:23,979 --> 01:09:26,234
Truth is, I don't want to know.

875
01:09:26,441 --> 01:09:28,902
Some things are best left unsaid.

876
01:09:32,488 --> 01:09:35,242
I like to think it was something so beautiful...

877
01:09:35,451 --> 01:09:37,577
...it can't be expressed in words...

878
01:09:37,786 --> 01:09:40,665
...and makes your heart ache because of it.

879
01:09:42,332 --> 01:09:44,836
I tell you, those voices soared...

880
01:09:45,127 --> 01:09:49,421
...higher and farther than anybody in a gray place dares to dream.

881
01:09:49,631 --> 01:09:52,968
It was like a beautiful bird flapped into our drab cage...

882
01:09:53,176 --> 01:09:55,972
...and made those walls dissolve away.

883
01:09:56,389 --> 01:09:58,598
And for the briefest of moments...

884
01:09:58,807 --> 01:10:01,768
...every last man at Shawshank felt free.

885
01:10:04,354 --> 01:10:07,023
It pissed the warden off something awful.

886
01:10:08,316 --> 01:10:09,317
Open the door.

887
01:10:12,237 --> 01:10:13,989
Open it up!

888
01:10:15,323 --> 01:10:18,034
Dufresne, open this door!

889
01:10:19,369 --> 01:10:20,579
Turn that off!

890
01:10:27,669 --> 01:10:30,088
I am warning you. Turn that off!

891
01:10:47,856 --> 01:10:49,357
You're mine now.

892
01:10:56,823 --> 01:11:00,035
Andy got two weeks in the hole for that little stunt.

893
01:11:00,243 --> 01:11:01,827
On your feet.

894
01:11:03,038 --> 01:11:05,415
- Hey, look who's here. - Maestro!

895
01:11:07,209 --> 01:11:10,837
You couldn't play something good, huh? Like Hank Williams?

896
01:11:11,212 --> 01:11:14,007
They broke the door down before I could take requests.

897
01:11:14,216 --> 01:11:17,344
- Was it worth two weeks? - Easiest time I ever did.

898
01:11:17,551 --> 01:11:21,723
- No such thing as easy time in the hole. - A week in the hole is like a year.

899
01:11:21,932 --> 01:11:25,226
- Damn straight. - I had Mr. Mozart to keep me company.

900
01:11:25,435 --> 01:11:28,395
So they let you tote that record player down there, huh?

901
01:11:30,856 --> 01:11:32,234
It was in here.

902
01:11:33,068 --> 01:11:34,277
In here.

903
01:11:35,403 --> 01:11:37,531
That's the beauty of music. They...

904
01:11:37,739 --> 01:11:40,075
...can't get that from you.

905
01:11:43,577 --> 01:11:45,956
Haven't you ever felt that way about music?

906
01:11:47,748 --> 01:11:50,877
Well, I played a mean harmonica as a younger man.

907
01:11:51,919 --> 01:11:53,630
Lost interest in it, though.

908
01:11:53,838 --> 01:11:55,422
Didn't make much sense in here.

909
01:11:55,757 --> 01:11:57,759
Here's where it makes the most sense.

910
01:11:58,300 --> 01:12:00,387
You need it so you don't forget.

911
01:12:00,928 --> 01:12:02,139
Forget?

912
01:12:02,513 --> 01:12:05,600
Forget that there are...

913
01:12:05,808 --> 01:12:07,268
...places...

914
01:12:07,769 --> 01:12:11,605
...in the world that aren't made out of stone.

915
01:12:12,606 --> 01:12:14,066
There's something...

916
01:12:14,441 --> 01:12:15,819
...inside...

917
01:12:16,111 --> 01:12:18,070
...that they can't get to...

918
01:12:18,280 --> 01:12:19,614
...that they can't touch.

919
01:12:20,157 --> 01:12:21,616
That's yours.

920
01:12:23,117 --> 01:12:24,743
What are you talking about?

921
01:12:25,787 --> 01:12:26,997
Hope.

922
01:12:28,080 --> 01:12:29,416
Hope.

923
01:12:31,333 --> 01:12:33,336
Let me tell you something, my friend.

924
01:12:34,128 --> 01:12:36,464
Hope is a dangerous thing.

925
01:12:37,632 --> 01:12:39,968
Hope can drive a man insane.

926
01:12:40,677 --> 01:12:42,803
It's got no use on the inside.

927
01:12:43,013 --> 01:12:45,222
You'd better get used to that idea.

928
01:12:47,725 --> 01:12:49,227
Like Brooks did?

929
01:13:14,585 --> 01:13:15,586
Sit down.

930
01:13:20,925 --> 01:13:24,220
Says here that you've served 30 years of a life sentence.

931
01:13:25,387 --> 01:13:27,473
You feel you've been rehabilitated?

932
01:13:27,681 --> 01:13:28,891
Oh, yes, sir.

933
01:13:30,184 --> 01:13:31,519
Without a doubt.

934
01:13:32,687 --> 01:13:35,064
I can honestly say I'm a changed man.

935
01:13:37,066 --> 01:13:39,026
No danger to society here.

936
01:13:39,527 --> 01:13:41,069
God's honest truth.

937
01:13:43,656 --> 01:13:45,741
Absolutely rehabilitated.

938
01:13:55,751 --> 01:13:57,086
Thirty years.

939
01:13:59,046 --> 01:14:00,965
Jesus, when you say it like that...

940
01:14:01,799 --> 01:14:03,634
You wonder where it went.

941
01:14:06,052 --> 01:14:08,221
I wonder where 10 years went.

942
01:14:13,185 --> 01:14:14,228
Here.

943
01:14:14,645 --> 01:14:17,398
A little parole rejection present.

944
01:14:18,649 --> 01:14:20,358
Go ahead and open it.

945
01:14:21,485 --> 01:14:23,487
Went through one of your competitors.

946
01:14:23,695 --> 01:14:26,824
I hope you don't mind. I wanted it to be a surprise.

947
01:14:36,876 --> 01:14:38,377
It's very pretty.

948
01:14:40,087 --> 01:14:41,296
Thank you.

949
01:14:43,841 --> 01:14:45,341
You going to play it?

950
01:14:49,889 --> 01:14:51,139
No.

951
01:14:54,768 --> 01:14:56,186
Not right now.

952
01:15:13,871 --> 01:15:14,954
Roll in!

953
01:15:51,158 --> 01:15:52,367
Lights out!

954
01:16:26,985 --> 01:16:29,112
Andy was as good as his word.

955
01:16:29,487 --> 01:16:32,282
He wrote two letters a week instead of one.

956
01:16:34,326 --> 01:16:38,413
In 1959, the state senate finally clued in to the fact...

957
01:16:38,664 --> 01:16:41,791
...they couldn't buy him off with just a $200 check.

958
01:16:42,000 --> 01:16:46,296
Appropriations Committee voted an annual payment of $500...

959
01:16:46,504 --> 01:16:48,423
...just to shut him up.

960
01:16:48,631 --> 01:16:51,593
And you'd be amazed how far Andy could stretch it.

961
01:16:51,800 --> 01:16:54,762
He made deals with book clubs, charity groups.

962
01:16:54,971 --> 01:16:57,723
He bought remaindered books by the pound...

963
01:16:58,224 --> 01:16:59,767
Treasure Island.

964
01:17:00,310 --> 01:17:01,644
Robert Louis-

965
01:17:01,811 --> 01:17:02,854
Stevenson.

966
01:17:03,061 --> 01:17:04,439
Fiction, adventure.

967
01:17:06,983 --> 01:17:08,026
What's next?

968
01:17:08,234 --> 01:17:11,279
I got here Auto Repair...

969
01:17:12,029 --> 01:17:13,197
...and Soap Carving.

970
01:17:13,406 --> 01:17:16,618
Trade skills and hobbies. Under "Educational," behind you.

971
01:17:16,826 --> 01:17:18,703
Count of Monte Crisco.

972
01:17:19,162 --> 01:17:21,498
That's "Cristo," you dumb shit.

973
01:17:22,581 --> 01:17:23,582
By Alexandree...

974
01:17:24,042 --> 01:17:25,293
...Dum-ass.

975
01:17:25,668 --> 01:17:26,669
Dumb ass.

976
01:17:29,005 --> 01:17:30,048
Dumb ass?

977
01:17:32,966 --> 01:17:34,928
Dumas. Know what that's about?

978
01:17:37,179 --> 01:17:39,181
You'd like it. It's about a prison break.

979
01:17:39,348 --> 01:17:43,269
We ought to file that under "Educational" too, oughtn't we?

980
01:17:44,145 --> 01:17:48,233
The rest of us did our best to pitch in when and where we could.

981
01:17:48,817 --> 01:17:50,567
By the year Kennedy was shot...

982
01:17:50,819 --> 01:17:55,323
...Andy had transformed a storage room smelling of turpentine...

983
01:17:55,615 --> 01:17:58,700
...into the best prison library in New England...

984
01:17:59,409 --> 01:18:02,372
...complete with a fine selection of Hank Williams.

985
01:18:09,712 --> 01:18:11,672
That was also when Warden Norton...

986
01:18:11,881 --> 01:18:14,801
...instituted his famous "Inside Out" program.

987
01:18:15,259 --> 01:18:17,344
You may remember reading about it.

988
01:18:17,554 --> 01:18:20,682
It made the papers and got his picture in Look magazine.

989
01:18:20,889 --> 01:18:22,183
It's no free ride...

990
01:18:22,391 --> 01:18:25,603
.. but rather a genuine progressive advance...

991
01:18:25,811 --> 01:18:28,313
...in corrections and rehabilitation.

992
01:18:28,648 --> 01:18:31,191
Our inmates, properly supervised...

993
01:18:31,401 --> 01:18:34,027
...will be put to work outside these walls...

994
01:18:34,236 --> 01:18:37,781
...performing all manner of public service.

995
01:18:38,073 --> 01:18:40,284
These men can learn the value...

996
01:18:40,493 --> 01:18:44,288
...of an honest day's labor and provide a service to the community...

997
01:18:44,496 --> 01:18:49,209
...at a bare minimum of expense to Mr. and Mrs. John Q. Taxpayer.

998
01:18:49,626 --> 01:18:51,962
Of course he didn't tell the press...

999
01:18:52,171 --> 01:18:55,632
...that "bare minimum of expense" is a fairly loose term.

1000
01:18:55,841 --> 01:18:58,886
There are 100 different ways to skim off the top.

1001
01:18:59,094 --> 01:19:01,555
Men, materials, you name it.

1002
01:19:01,930 --> 01:19:04,808
And oh, my Lord, how the money rolled in!

1003
01:19:05,017 --> 01:19:07,561
At this rate, you'll put me out of business.

1004
01:19:07,769 --> 01:19:12,191
With this pool of slave labor, you can underbid any contractor in town.

1005
01:19:12,774 --> 01:19:15,737
We're providing a valuable community service.

1006
01:19:15,944 --> 01:19:19,573
That's fine for the papers, but I've got a family to feed.

1007
01:19:22,284 --> 01:19:23,786
We go back a long way.

1008
01:19:25,120 --> 01:19:30,043
I need this highway contract. I don't get it and I go under. That's a fact.

1009
01:19:30,959 --> 01:19:32,753
You have some of this fine pie...

1010
01:19:32,961 --> 01:19:36,298
...my missus made for you. You think about that.

1011
01:19:44,598 --> 01:19:47,267
I wouldn't worry too much about this contract.

1012
01:19:47,517 --> 01:19:50,604
I already got my boys committed elsewhere.

1013
01:19:52,564 --> 01:19:55,608
You be sure and thank Maisie for this fine pie.

1014
01:19:56,735 --> 01:19:58,904
And behind every shady deal...

1015
01:19:59,238 --> 01:20:01,865
...behind every dollar earned...

1016
01:20:02,157 --> 01:20:05,202
...there was Andy, keeping the books.

1017
01:20:05,619 --> 01:20:06,869
Two deposits.

1018
01:20:07,121 --> 01:20:11,250
Maine National and New England First. Night drops as always, sir.

1019
01:20:41,447 --> 01:20:45,784
Get my stuff to the laundry. Two suits for dry-clean and a bag of whatnot.

1020
01:20:46,243 --> 01:20:49,787
If they over-starch my shirts again, they'll hear from me.

1021
01:20:49,997 --> 01:20:51,330
How do I look?

1022
01:20:52,373 --> 01:20:54,834
- Very nice. - Big charity to-do up Portland way.

1023
01:20:55,043 --> 01:20:56,586
Governor will be there.

1024
01:20:58,004 --> 01:20:59,464
You want the rest of this?

1025
01:21:00,799 --> 01:21:02,718
Woman can't bake worth shit.

1026
01:21:03,969 --> 01:21:05,304
Thank you, sir.

1027
01:21:08,139 --> 01:21:11,351
He's got his fingers in a lot of pies, from what I hear.

1028
01:21:11,559 --> 01:21:16,398
He's got scams you haven't even dreamed of. Kickbacks on his kickbacks.

1029
01:21:16,606 --> 01:21:18,983
A river of dirty money running through here.

1030
01:21:19,193 --> 01:21:23,029
Sooner or later, he'll have to explain where it came from.

1031
01:21:23,238 --> 01:21:24,989
That's where I come in.

1032
01:21:25,573 --> 01:21:27,909
I channel it. Filter it. Funnel it.

1033
01:21:28,075 --> 01:21:30,912
Stocks, securities, tax-free municipals.

1034
01:21:31,245 --> 01:21:34,792
I send it out into the real world, and when it comes back...

1035
01:21:34,999 --> 01:21:37,961
- Clean as a virgin's honeypot, huh? - Cleaner.

1036
01:21:38,586 --> 01:21:42,173
By the time Norton retires, I'll have made him a millionaire.

1037
01:21:44,509 --> 01:21:48,846
If they ever catch on, he'll wind up in here wearing a number himself.

1038
01:21:49,096 --> 01:21:51,809
I thought you had more faith in me than that.

1039
01:21:52,016 --> 01:21:55,061
I know you're good, but all that paper leaves a trail.

1040
01:21:55,270 --> 01:21:58,648
Now anybody gets curious, FBI, IRS...

1041
01:21:58,856 --> 01:21:59,857
...whatever.

1042
01:22:00,274 --> 01:22:01,609
It'll lead to somebody.

1043
01:22:01,777 --> 01:22:05,655
Sure it is, but not to me, and certainly not to the warden.

1044
01:22:10,034 --> 01:22:11,035
All right, who?

1045
01:22:11,244 --> 01:22:12,829
Randall Stevens.

1046
01:22:13,371 --> 01:22:14,288
Who?

1047
01:22:14,498 --> 01:22:16,499
The "silent" silent partner.

1048
01:22:16,707 --> 01:22:19,752
He's the guilty one, the man with the bank accounts.

1049
01:22:19,961 --> 01:22:22,130
It's where the filtering process starts.

1050
01:22:22,338 --> 01:22:25,216
They trace anything, it'll just lead to him.

1051
01:22:25,800 --> 01:22:27,052
But who is he?

1052
01:22:27,260 --> 01:22:31,222
He's a phantom, an apparition. Second cousin to Harvey the Rabbit.

1053
01:22:32,306 --> 01:22:33,474
I conjured him...

1054
01:22:33,683 --> 01:22:35,059
...out of thin air.

1055
01:22:35,852 --> 01:22:38,604
He doesn't exist, except on paper.

1056
01:22:41,649 --> 01:22:44,026
You can't just make a person up.

1057
01:22:44,235 --> 01:22:47,321
Sure you can, if you know how the system works.

1058
01:22:47,822 --> 01:22:50,658
It's amazing what you can accomplish by mail.

1059
01:22:50,992 --> 01:22:53,619
Mr. Stevens has a birth certificate...

1060
01:22:53,828 --> 01:22:55,955
...driver's license, Social Security.

1061
01:22:56,247 --> 01:22:57,247
You're shitting me.

1062
01:22:57,457 --> 01:23:00,835
If they trace any accounts, they'll wind up chasing...

1063
01:23:01,001 --> 01:23:02,753
...a figment of my imagination.

1064
01:23:02,962 --> 01:23:04,964
Well, I'll be damned!

1065
01:23:06,299 --> 01:23:08,009
Did I say you were good?

1066
01:23:08,384 --> 01:23:10,011
Shit, you are Rembrandt.

1067
01:23:10,761 --> 01:23:12,472
The funny thing is...

1068
01:23:12,680 --> 01:23:16,809
...on the outside, I was an honest man, straight as an arrow.

1069
01:23:17,184 --> 01:23:19,520
I had to come to prison to be a crook.

1070
01:23:28,404 --> 01:23:29,697
Ever bother you?

1071
01:23:31,157 --> 01:23:34,201
I don't run the scams. I just process the profits.

1072
01:23:34,493 --> 01:23:36,496
A fine line, maybe...

1073
01:23:37,038 --> 01:23:39,122
...but I also built that library...

1074
01:23:39,332 --> 01:23:42,334
...and used it to help guys get their high school diploma.

1075
01:23:42,543 --> 01:23:44,796
Why do you think he lets me do all that?

1076
01:23:45,004 --> 01:23:47,173
Keep you happy and doing the laundry.

1077
01:23:47,380 --> 01:23:48,883
Money instead of sheets.

1078
01:23:49,216 --> 01:23:51,843
Well, I work cheap. That's the tradeoff.

1079
01:24:09,070 --> 01:24:11,697
Tommy Williams came to Shawshank in 1965...

1080
01:24:11,947 --> 01:24:14,199
...on a two-year stretch for B and E.

1081
01:24:14,407 --> 01:24:16,744
That's breaking and entering to you.

1082
01:24:17,036 --> 01:24:21,249
Cops caught him sneaking TV sets out the back door of a JC Penney.

1083
01:24:21,706 --> 01:24:22,999
Young punk.

1084
01:24:23,208 --> 01:24:24,836
Mr. Rock 'n' Roll...

1085
01:24:25,043 --> 01:24:26,545
...cocky as hell.

1086
01:24:26,754 --> 01:24:29,173
Come on, old boys. Moving like molasses!

1087
01:24:29,422 --> 01:24:31,175
Making me look bad.

1088
01:24:31,383 --> 01:24:33,176
We liked him immediately.

1089
01:24:33,426 --> 01:24:37,140
I'm backing out the door and I got the TV like this.

1090
01:24:37,431 --> 01:24:41,059
A big old thing. I couldn't see shit. Then I hear this voice.

1091
01:24:41,727 --> 01:24:43,687
"Freeze, kid, hands in the air."

1092
01:24:43,896 --> 01:24:48,358
I was standing there, holding onto that TV. Finally the voice says:

1093
01:24:48,567 --> 01:24:52,362
"You hear what I said, boy?" I say, "Yes, sir. I did.

1094
01:24:52,572 --> 01:24:56,033
But if I drop this, you get me on destruction of property too."

1095
01:25:00,495 --> 01:25:02,789
You done some stretch in Cashman, right?

1096
01:25:03,290 --> 01:25:07,252
Yeah, that was an easy piece of time, let me tell you.

1097
01:25:07,544 --> 01:25:09,463
Weekend furloughs. Work programs.

1098
01:25:09,671 --> 01:25:10,755
Not like here.

1099
01:25:10,964 --> 01:25:13,384
Sounds like you done time all over.

1100
01:25:13,800 --> 01:25:16,219
I've been in and out since I was 13.

1101
01:25:16,428 --> 01:25:18,597
Name it, chances are I've been there.

1102
01:25:18,805 --> 01:25:21,476
Perhaps you should try a new profession.

1103
01:25:22,934 --> 01:25:24,478
What I mean is...

1104
01:25:24,811 --> 01:25:28,273
...you're not a very good thief. You should try something else.

1105
01:25:28,482 --> 01:25:31,735
Yeah, what the hell you know about it, Capone?

1106
01:25:32,152 --> 01:25:33,737
What are you in for?

1107
01:25:33,987 --> 01:25:34,988
Me?

1108
01:25:37,449 --> 01:25:39,117
A lawyer fucked me.

1109
01:25:42,996 --> 01:25:45,999
Everybody's innocent in here. Don't you know that?

1110
01:25:53,882 --> 01:25:58,302
As it turned out, Tommy had himself a young wife and a new baby girl.

1111
01:25:58,845 --> 01:26:00,972
Maybe he thought of them on the streets...

1112
01:26:01,180 --> 01:26:03,683
...or his child growing up not knowing her daddy.

1113
01:26:04,017 --> 01:26:05,352
Whatever it was...

1114
01:26:05,685 --> 01:26:08,146
...something lit a fire under that boy's ass.

1115
01:26:11,983 --> 01:26:14,861
Thought I might try for my high school equivalency.

1116
01:26:15,028 --> 01:26:17,572
Hear you helped a couple of fellas with that.

1117
01:26:18,907 --> 01:26:21,159
I don't waste time with losers, Tommy.

1118
01:26:22,536 --> 01:26:25,037
I ain't no goddamn loser.

1119
01:26:26,790 --> 01:26:28,624
- You mean that? - Yeah.

1120
01:26:30,293 --> 01:26:31,711
You really mean that?

1121
01:26:32,754 --> 01:26:34,130
Yes, sir, I do.

1122
01:26:34,339 --> 01:26:36,298
Good. Because if we do this...

1123
01:26:36,508 --> 01:26:40,469
...we do it all the way, a hundred percent, nothing half-assed.

1124
01:26:40,720 --> 01:26:42,513
Thing is, see...

1125
01:26:43,765 --> 01:26:45,308
...I don't read so good.

1126
01:26:46,476 --> 01:26:47,560
"Well."

1127
01:26:49,562 --> 01:26:51,313
You don't read...

1128
01:26:51,565 --> 01:26:53,065
...so well.

1129
01:26:55,402 --> 01:26:56,903
We'll get to that.

1130
01:27:01,366 --> 01:27:03,493
So Andy took Tommy under his wing.

1131
01:27:03,701 --> 01:27:06,328
Started walking him through his ABC's.

1132
01:27:08,457 --> 01:27:10,666
Tommy took to it pretty well too.

1133
01:27:10,916 --> 01:27:13,711
Boy found brains he never knew he had.

1134
01:27:16,046 --> 01:27:19,467
Before long, Andy started him on his course requirements.

1135
01:27:19,676 --> 01:27:21,343
He really liked the kid.

1136
01:27:21,553 --> 01:27:25,097
Gave him a thrill to help a youngster crawl off the shit heap.

1137
01:27:25,599 --> 01:27:27,892
But that wasn't the only reason.

1138
01:27:28,601 --> 01:27:30,769
Prison time is slow time.

1139
01:27:31,271 --> 01:27:33,857
So you do what you can to keep going.

1140
01:27:34,190 --> 01:27:36,233
Some fellas collect stamps.

1141
01:27:36,650 --> 01:27:39,111
Others build matchstick houses.

1142
01:27:39,737 --> 01:27:42,406
Andy built a library.

1143
01:27:42,907 --> 01:27:44,826
Now he needed a new project.

1144
01:27:45,702 --> 01:27:47,203
Tommy was it.

1145
01:27:47,828 --> 01:27:51,832
It was the same reason he spent years shaping and polishing those rocks.

1146
01:27:52,374 --> 01:27:55,586
The same reason he hung his fantasy girlies on the wall.

1147
01:27:56,504 --> 01:27:57,755
In prison...

1148
01:27:58,047 --> 01:28:01,759
...a man will do most anything to keep his mind occupied.

1149
01:28:02,551 --> 01:28:07,431
By 1966, right about the time Tommy was getting ready to take his exams...

1150
01:28:07,641 --> 01:28:09,850
...it was lovely Raquel.

1151
01:28:26,408 --> 01:28:27,409
Time.

1152
01:28:33,457 --> 01:28:34,458
Well?

1153
01:28:36,043 --> 01:28:38,170
Well, it's for shit.

1154
01:28:38,671 --> 01:28:41,507
I wasted a whole year of my time with this bullshit.

1155
01:28:41,715 --> 01:28:43,217
It's probably not that bad.

1156
01:28:43,425 --> 01:28:47,638
I didn't get a thing right. It might as well have been in Chinese.

1157
01:28:47,847 --> 01:28:49,598
Let's see how the score comes out.

1158
01:28:49,807 --> 01:28:52,768
I'll tell you how the goddamn score comes out.

1159
01:28:54,687 --> 01:28:56,063
Two points, right there!

1160
01:28:56,272 --> 01:28:57,815
There's your goddamn score!

1161
01:28:58,064 --> 01:29:01,777
Goddamn cats crawling up trees, 5 times 5 is 25...

1162
01:29:02,695 --> 01:29:04,321
Fuck this place!

1163
01:29:04,571 --> 01:29:05,698
Fuck it!

1164
01:29:24,884 --> 01:29:26,135
I feel bad.

1165
01:29:26,343 --> 01:29:27,510
I let him down.

1166
01:29:27,720 --> 01:29:29,472
That's crap, kid.

1167
01:29:29,679 --> 01:29:31,014
He's proud of you.

1168
01:29:31,348 --> 01:29:34,226
We're old friends, I know him as good as anybody.

1169
01:29:34,393 --> 01:29:35,978
Smart fellow, ain't he?

1170
01:29:36,186 --> 01:29:39,315
Smart as they come. He was a banker on the outside.

1171
01:29:39,522 --> 01:29:41,734
What's he in here for anyway?

1172
01:29:42,067 --> 01:29:43,068
Murder.

1173
01:29:44,528 --> 01:29:45,905
The hell, you say.

1174
01:29:48,031 --> 01:29:50,366
You wouldn't think it to look at the guy.

1175
01:29:51,577 --> 01:29:55,705
Caught his wife in bed with some golf pro. Greased them both.

1176
01:30:03,714 --> 01:30:04,715
What?

1177
01:30:06,050 --> 01:30:08,594
About four years ago...

1178
01:30:08,886 --> 01:30:12,097
...I was in Thomaston on a two-to-three stretch.

1179
01:30:12,597 --> 01:30:13,933
I stole a car.

1180
01:30:14,266 --> 01:30:16,268
It was a dumb-fuck thing to do.

1181
01:30:16,935 --> 01:30:19,188
About six months left to go...

1182
01:30:19,480 --> 01:30:21,397
...I get a new cellmate in.

1183
01:30:22,107 --> 01:30:23,733
Elmo Blatch.

1184
01:30:24,442 --> 01:30:25,777
Big, twitchy fucker.

1185
01:30:26,611 --> 01:30:30,115
Kind of roomie you pray you don't get. You know what I'm saying?

1186
01:30:30,323 --> 01:30:33,076
Six-to-twelve, armed burglary.

1187
01:30:33,327 --> 01:30:35,578
Said he pulled hundreds of jobs.

1188
01:30:35,788 --> 01:30:40,750
Hard to believe, high-strung as he was. You cut a loud fart, he jumped three feet.

1189
01:30:41,793 --> 01:30:46,422
Talked all the time too. That's the other thing. He never shut up.

1190
01:30:46,631 --> 01:30:48,258
Places he'd been in...

1191
01:30:48,591 --> 01:30:51,636
...jobs he'd pulled, women he fucked.

1192
01:30:51,886 --> 01:30:54,097
Even people he killed.

1193
01:30:55,306 --> 01:30:58,143
People who "gave him shit."

1194
01:30:58,768 --> 01:31:00,311
That's how he put it.

1195
01:31:02,940 --> 01:31:04,983
So one night, like a joke...

1196
01:31:06,110 --> 01:31:09,612
...I say to him, "Elmo, who did you kill?"

1197
01:31:09,821 --> 01:31:10,947
So he says:

1198
01:31:11,157 --> 01:31:15,118
I got me this job one time, busing tables at a country club...

1199
01:31:15,786 --> 01:31:19,664
...so I could case all these big rich pricks that come in.

1200
01:31:21,791 --> 01:31:23,793
So I pick out this guy...

1201
01:31:24,669 --> 01:31:26,254
...go in one night...

1202
01:31:26,463 --> 01:31:28,173
...and do his place.

1203
01:31:29,632 --> 01:31:30,967
He wakes up...

1204
01:31:31,677 --> 01:31:33,470
...and gives me shit.

1205
01:31:34,512 --> 01:31:36,181
So I killed him.

1206
01:31:37,974 --> 01:31:40,810
Him and this tasty bitch he was with.

1207
01:31:42,187 --> 01:31:44,481
And that's the best part.

1208
01:31:45,148 --> 01:31:47,609
She's fucking this prick, see...

1209
01:31:47,817 --> 01:31:50,862
...this golf pro, but she's married to some other guy.

1210
01:31:51,488 --> 01:31:54,032
Some hotshot banker.

1211
01:31:55,492 --> 01:31:57,994
And he's the one they pinned it on.

1212
01:32:09,464 --> 01:32:13,343
I have to say that's the most amazing story I ever heard.

1213
01:32:14,594 --> 01:32:17,096
What amazes me most is you'd be taken in by it.

1214
01:32:17,847 --> 01:32:18,807
Sir?

1215
01:32:21,058 --> 01:32:24,646
It's obvious this fellow Williams is impressed with you.

1216
01:32:25,230 --> 01:32:29,192
He hears your tale of woe and naturally wants to cheer you up.

1217
01:32:29,400 --> 01:32:31,528
He's young, not terribly bright.

1218
01:32:32,069 --> 01:32:35,531
It's not surprising he wouldn't know what a state he put you in.

1219
01:32:35,907 --> 01:32:37,492
Sir, he's telling the truth.

1220
01:32:38,242 --> 01:32:42,413
Let's say for the moment this Blatch does exist.

1221
01:32:42,705 --> 01:32:47,334
You think he'd just fall to his knees and cry, "Yes, I did it. I confess.

1222
01:32:47,544 --> 01:32:50,213
By the way, add a life term to my sentence."

1223
01:32:50,421 --> 01:32:53,257
With Tommy's testimony, I can get a new trial.

1224
01:32:53,423 --> 01:32:55,927
That's assuming Blatch is still there.

1225
01:32:56,136 --> 01:32:58,096
Chances are, he'd be released by now.

1226
01:32:58,263 --> 01:33:00,764
They'd have his last known address.

1227
01:33:00,931 --> 01:33:02,892
It's a chance, isn't it?

1228
01:33:05,228 --> 01:33:06,938
How can you be so obtuse?

1229
01:33:07,896 --> 01:33:08,897
What?

1230
01:33:10,400 --> 01:33:11,860
What did you call me?

1231
01:33:12,067 --> 01:33:13,777
Obtuse. Is it deliberate?

1232
01:33:14,154 --> 01:33:15,654
You're forgetting yourself.

1233
01:33:15,988 --> 01:33:18,950
The country club will have his old timecards.

1234
01:33:19,116 --> 01:33:21,910
Records, W-2s with his name on them.

1235
01:33:22,120 --> 01:33:24,788
If you want to indulge this fantasy, it's your business.

1236
01:33:24,998 --> 01:33:27,584
Don't make it mine. This meeting is over.

1237
01:33:27,791 --> 01:33:30,419
If I got out, I'd never mention what happens here.

1238
01:33:31,045 --> 01:33:34,006
I'd be as indictable as you for laundering that money.

1239
01:33:35,883 --> 01:33:38,802
Don't ever mention money to me, you son of a bitch!

1240
01:33:39,012 --> 01:33:40,679
Not in this office...

1241
01:33:40,889 --> 01:33:42,806
...not anywhere. Get in here, now!

1242
01:33:43,057 --> 01:33:45,768
I just wanted to put you at ease, that's all.

1243
01:33:46,393 --> 01:33:47,645
Solitary. A month.

1244
01:33:48,645 --> 01:33:49,938
What's wrong with you?

1245
01:33:50,147 --> 01:33:51,482
Get him out of here.

1246
01:33:51,648 --> 01:33:53,233
This is my chance to get out!

1247
01:33:53,443 --> 01:33:55,235
It's my life! Understand?!

1248
01:33:55,444 --> 01:33:57,489
Get him out!

1249
01:33:59,281 --> 01:34:01,242
A month in the hole.

1250
01:34:01,450 --> 01:34:04,120
That's the longest stretch I ever heard of.

1251
01:34:04,328 --> 01:34:05,662
It's all my fault.

1252
01:34:05,829 --> 01:34:07,457
Bullshit.

1253
01:34:08,123 --> 01:34:10,793
You didn't pull the trigger or convict him.

1254
01:34:11,001 --> 01:34:13,420
Are you saying Andy is innocent?

1255
01:34:13,629 --> 01:34:15,631
I mean, for real innocent?

1256
01:34:15,840 --> 01:34:17,174
It looks that way.

1257
01:34:17,633 --> 01:34:19,510
Sweet Jesus.

1258
01:34:19,802 --> 01:34:21,637
How long has he been here now?

1259
01:34:22,012 --> 01:34:25,307
1947. What is that? Nineteen years.

1260
01:34:25,517 --> 01:34:28,143
- Williams, Thomas. - Yeah, over here.

1261
01:34:33,982 --> 01:34:35,150
What you got?

1262
01:34:35,776 --> 01:34:37,111
Board of Education.

1263
01:34:37,319 --> 01:34:38,821
That son of a bitch mailed it.

1264
01:34:39,029 --> 01:34:42,991
You going to open it or stand there with your thumb up your butt?

1265
01:34:43,200 --> 01:34:45,369
Thumb up my butt sounds better.

1266
01:34:45,868 --> 01:34:48,705
Skeets, come on. Give me that, you shithead.

1267
01:34:49,540 --> 01:34:50,874
Floyd, come on.

1268
01:34:56,338 --> 01:34:58,715
Will you throw that away, please?

1269
01:35:04,346 --> 01:35:06,056
Well, shit.

1270
01:35:18,735 --> 01:35:21,530
The kid passed. C + average.

1271
01:35:21,738 --> 01:35:23,865
Thought you'd like to know.

1272
01:35:40,090 --> 01:35:41,550
Warden wants to talk.

1273
01:35:55,272 --> 01:35:56,355
Out here?

1274
01:35:56,565 --> 01:35:58,776
That's what the man said.

1275
01:36:14,917 --> 01:36:15,918
Warden?

1276
01:36:25,928 --> 01:36:29,972
I'm asking you to keep this conversation just between us.

1277
01:36:31,474 --> 01:36:33,768
I feel awkward enough as it is.

1278
01:36:52,204 --> 01:36:54,288
We got a situation here.

1279
01:36:55,164 --> 01:36:57,251
I think you can appreciate that.

1280
01:36:57,458 --> 01:36:58,668
Yes, sir.

1281
01:36:58,960 --> 01:37:00,336
I sure can.

1282
01:37:00,628 --> 01:37:04,632
I tell you, son, this thing really came along and knocked my wind out.

1283
01:37:06,134 --> 01:37:08,678
It's got me up nights. That's the truth.

1284
01:37:10,973 --> 01:37:12,807
The right thing to do...

1285
01:37:14,475 --> 01:37:18,188
...sometimes it's hard to know what that is.

1286
01:37:19,188 --> 01:37:20,649
Do you understand?

1287
01:37:22,942 --> 01:37:24,527
I need your help, son.

1288
01:37:25,987 --> 01:37:28,157
If I'm going to move on this...

1289
01:37:28,489 --> 01:37:31,659
...there can't be the least little shred of doubt.

1290
01:37:32,368 --> 01:37:33,453
I have to know...

1291
01:37:33,661 --> 01:37:36,289
...if what you told Dufresne was the truth.

1292
01:37:36,497 --> 01:37:37,623
Yes, sir.

1293
01:37:37,831 --> 01:37:39,041
Absolutely.

1294
01:37:39,542 --> 01:37:42,545
Would you be willing to swear before a judge and jury...

1295
01:37:42,753 --> 01:37:44,755
...with your hand on the Good Book...

1296
01:37:44,964 --> 01:37:47,633
...and take an oath before Almighty God himself?

1297
01:37:47,842 --> 01:37:49,635
Just give me that chance.

1298
01:37:53,890 --> 01:37:55,224
That's what I thought.

1299
01:38:42,313 --> 01:38:44,232
I'm sure by now you've heard.

1300
01:38:45,982 --> 01:38:47,025
Terrible thing.

1301
01:38:48,610 --> 01:38:50,113
A man that young...

1302
01:38:50,446 --> 01:38:53,032
...less than a year to go, trying to escape.

1303
01:38:53,574 --> 01:38:56,077
Broke Captain Hadley's heart to shoot him.

1304
01:38:56,786 --> 01:38:58,286
Truly, it did.

1305
01:39:01,289 --> 01:39:03,750
We just have to put it behind us.

1306
01:39:05,419 --> 01:39:06,629
Move on.

1307
01:39:08,255 --> 01:39:09,757
I'm done.

1308
01:39:11,050 --> 01:39:12,677
Everything stops.

1309
01:39:14,636 --> 01:39:16,973
Get someone else to run your scams.

1310
01:39:18,266 --> 01:39:20,100
Nothing stops.

1311
01:39:21,435 --> 01:39:22,603
Nothing.

1312
01:39:25,981 --> 01:39:29,110
Or you will do the hardest time there is.

1313
01:39:29,484 --> 01:39:31,820
No more protection from the guards.

1314
01:39:32,029 --> 01:39:36,950
I'll pull you out of that 1-bunk Hilton and cast you down with the sodomites.

1315
01:39:38,326 --> 01:39:40,787
You'll think you've been fucked by a train.

1316
01:39:42,164 --> 01:39:43,665
And the library?

1317
01:39:44,332 --> 01:39:45,333
Gone.

1318
01:39:45,959 --> 01:39:48,503
Sealed off, brick by brick.

1319
01:39:49,004 --> 01:39:51,631
We'll have us a little book barbecue in the yard.

1320
01:39:51,840 --> 01:39:53,968
They'll see the flames for miles.

1321
01:39:54,176 --> 01:39:57,013
We'll dance around it like wild Injuns.

1322
01:39:58,306 --> 01:40:01,308
You understand me? Catching my drift?

1323
01:40:05,020 --> 01:40:06,897
Or am I being obtuse?

1324
01:40:18,700 --> 01:40:21,328
Give him another month to think about it.

1325
01:41:08,082 --> 01:41:11,128
My wife used to say I'm a hard man to know.

1326
01:41:11,920 --> 01:41:13,755
Like a closed book.

1327
01:41:14,589 --> 01:41:16,590
Complained about it all the time.

1328
01:41:17,551 --> 01:41:19,094
She was beautiful.

1329
01:41:21,512 --> 01:41:23,056
God, I loved her.

1330
01:41:26,768 --> 01:41:28,979
I didn't know how to show it, that's all.

1331
01:41:31,730 --> 01:41:33,400
I killed her, Red.

1332
01:41:35,277 --> 01:41:37,070
I didn't pull the trigger...

1333
01:41:38,488 --> 01:41:40,240
...but I drove her away.

1334
01:41:41,740 --> 01:41:44,411
That's why she died, because of me...

1335
01:41:44,618 --> 01:41:45,996
...the way I am.

1336
01:41:55,004 --> 01:41:56,840
That don't make you a murderer.

1337
01:42:00,217 --> 01:42:01,969
Bad husband, maybe.

1338
01:42:05,640 --> 01:42:08,893
Feel bad about it if you want, but you didn't pull the trigger.

1339
01:42:09,102 --> 01:42:10,436
No, I didn't.

1340
01:42:10,812 --> 01:42:12,772
Somebody else did.

1341
01:42:13,982 --> 01:42:16,108
And I wound up in here.

1342
01:42:18,569 --> 01:42:20,154
Bad luck, I guess.

1343
01:42:25,367 --> 01:42:26,827
It floats around.

1344
01:42:27,828 --> 01:42:30,080
It's got to land on somebody.

1345
01:42:30,998 --> 01:42:32,875
It was my turn, that's all.

1346
01:42:33,584 --> 01:42:36,337
I was in the path of the tornado.

1347
01:42:42,302 --> 01:42:46,263
I just didn't expect the storm would last as long as it has.

1348
01:42:50,601 --> 01:42:52,311
Think you'll ever get out of here?

1349
01:42:53,521 --> 01:42:54,563
Me?

1350
01:42:57,525 --> 01:42:58,526
Yeah.

1351
01:42:59,860 --> 01:43:02,196
One day, when I got a long, white beard...

1352
01:43:02,363 --> 01:43:05,533
...and two or three marbles rolling around upstairs.

1353
01:43:07,576 --> 01:43:09,203
I tell you where I'd go.

1354
01:43:10,704 --> 01:43:12,331
Zihuatanejo.

1355
01:43:13,207 --> 01:43:14,416
Say what?

1356
01:43:14,708 --> 01:43:16,544
Zihuatanejo.

1357
01:43:17,503 --> 01:43:19,171
It's in Mexico.

1358
01:43:20,548 --> 01:43:22,675
A little place on the Pacific Ocean.

1359
01:43:23,801 --> 01:43:26,595
You know what the Mexicans say about the Pacific?

1360
01:43:28,389 --> 01:43:30,391
They say it has no memory.

1361
01:43:32,393 --> 01:43:35,020
That's where I want to live the rest of my life.

1362
01:43:36,689 --> 01:43:39,191
A warm place with no memory.

1363
01:43:42,402 --> 01:43:44,363
Open up a little hotel...

1364
01:43:46,073 --> 01:43:47,533
...right on the beach.

1365
01:43:48,159 --> 01:43:50,369
Buy some worthless old boat...

1366
01:43:50,911 --> 01:43:52,538
...and fix it up new.

1367
01:43:55,249 --> 01:43:56,500
Take my guests out...

1368
01:43:57,334 --> 01:43:58,711
...charter fishing.

1369
01:44:02,548 --> 01:44:04,258
Zihuatanejo.

1370
01:44:07,803 --> 01:44:11,682
In a place like that, I could use a man that knows how to get things.

1371
01:44:18,229 --> 01:44:21,066
I don't think I could make it on the outside.

1372
01:44:27,739 --> 01:44:29,783
I been in here most of my life.

1373
01:44:31,242 --> 01:44:32,995
I'm an institutional man now.

1374
01:44:33,913 --> 01:44:35,623
Just like Brooks was.

1375
01:44:37,123 --> 01:44:38,793
You underestimate yourself.

1376
01:44:39,376 --> 01:44:40,961
I don't think so.

1377
01:44:44,798 --> 01:44:48,761
In here I'm the guy who can get things for you, sure, but...

1378
01:44:49,512 --> 01:44:52,055
...outside all you need is the Yellow Pages.

1379
01:44:52,264 --> 01:44:54,474
Hell, I wouldn't know where to begin.

1380
01:44:56,643 --> 01:44:58,270
Pacific Ocean?

1381
01:44:58,645 --> 01:44:59,897
Shit.

1382
01:45:00,271 --> 01:45:02,440
Scare me to death, something that big.

1383
01:45:02,650 --> 01:45:03,943
Not me.

1384
01:45:05,151 --> 01:45:07,946
I didn't shoot my wife, and I didn't shoot her lover.

1385
01:45:09,656 --> 01:45:12,910
Whatever mistakes I made, I've paid for them and then some.

1386
01:45:13,952 --> 01:45:15,453
That hotel, that boat...

1387
01:45:17,206 --> 01:45:19,457
I don't think that's too much to ask.

1388
01:45:24,337 --> 01:45:26,798
You shouldn't be doing this to yourself.

1389
01:45:27,006 --> 01:45:29,008
This is just shitty pipe dreams.

1390
01:45:29,467 --> 01:45:31,928
Mexico is way down there and you're in here...

1391
01:45:32,138 --> 01:45:33,681
...and that's the way it is.

1392
01:45:34,013 --> 01:45:36,850
Yeah, right. That's the way it is.

1393
01:45:38,351 --> 01:45:40,771
It's down there and I'm in here.

1394
01:45:43,357 --> 01:45:46,067
I guess it comes down to a simple choice.

1395
01:45:48,987 --> 01:45:50,488
Get busy living...

1396
01:45:51,865 --> 01:45:53,867
...or get busy dying.

1397
01:46:06,004 --> 01:46:08,715
If you ever get out of here, do me a favor.

1398
01:46:08,923 --> 01:46:11,176
Sure, Andy. Anything.

1399
01:46:13,052 --> 01:46:16,931
There's a big hayfield up near Buxton. You know where Buxton is?

1400
01:46:18,391 --> 01:46:21,519
- A lot of hayfields up there. - One in particular.

1401
01:46:21,728 --> 01:46:25,690
It's got a long rock wall with a big oak tree at the north end.

1402
01:46:25,899 --> 01:46:28,736
It's like something out of a Robert Frost poem.

1403
01:46:29,694 --> 01:46:32,196
It's where I asked my wife to marry me.

1404
01:46:33,573 --> 01:46:35,700
We went there for a picnic...

1405
01:46:36,075 --> 01:46:38,203
...and made love under that oak...

1406
01:46:38,870 --> 01:46:41,080
...and I asked and she said yes.

1407
01:46:45,084 --> 01:46:46,920
Promise me, Red.

1408
01:46:47,754 --> 01:46:49,379
If you ever get out...

1409
01:46:49,589 --> 01:46:51,091
...find that spot.

1410
01:46:52,258 --> 01:46:57,097
At the base of that wall, there's a rock that has no earthly business in Maine.

1411
01:46:58,264 --> 01:47:01,267
Piece of black, volcanic glass.

1412
01:47:03,937 --> 01:47:06,606
Something's buried under it I want you to have.

1413
01:47:07,398 --> 01:47:08,607
What, Andy?

1414
01:47:09,067 --> 01:47:10,776
What's buried under there?

1415
01:47:12,403 --> 01:47:14,280
You'll have to pry it up...

1416
01:47:14,447 --> 01:47:15,740
...to see.

1417
01:47:21,621 --> 01:47:23,581
No, I'm telling you. The guy is...

1418
01:47:24,249 --> 01:47:25,959
He's talking funny.

1419
01:47:27,084 --> 01:47:28,753
I'm really worried about him.

1420
01:47:29,170 --> 01:47:30,630
Let's keep an eye on him.

1421
01:47:30,797 --> 01:47:34,092
That's fine during the day, but at night he's all alone.

1422
01:47:35,510 --> 01:47:36,635
Oh, Lord.

1423
01:47:37,971 --> 01:47:38,804
What?

1424
01:47:40,306 --> 01:47:43,100
Andy come down to the loading dock today.

1425
01:47:43,309 --> 01:47:45,395
He asked me for a length of rope.

1426
01:47:45,603 --> 01:47:46,437
Rope?

1427
01:47:46,646 --> 01:47:48,273
Six feet long.

1428
01:47:48,523 --> 01:47:49,898
And you gave it to him.

1429
01:47:50,774 --> 01:47:52,569
Sure. Why wouldn't I?

1430
01:47:52,776 --> 01:47:54,320
Jesus! Heywood.

1431
01:47:54,945 --> 01:47:56,906
How was I supposed to know?

1432
01:47:57,114 --> 01:47:58,658
Remember Brooks Hatlen?

1433
01:48:00,660 --> 01:48:02,619
Andy would never do that.

1434
01:48:03,203 --> 01:48:04,289
Never.

1435
01:48:06,332 --> 01:48:07,666
I don't know.

1436
01:48:10,503 --> 01:48:12,546
Every man has his breaking point.

1437
01:48:14,882 --> 01:48:17,135
Lickety-split. Want to get home.

1438
01:48:17,468 --> 01:48:19,219
Just about finished, sir.

1439
01:48:36,362 --> 01:48:37,988
Three deposits tonight.

1440
01:48:45,954 --> 01:48:47,873
Get my stuff down to the laundry.

1441
01:48:48,041 --> 01:48:49,666
And shine my shoes.

1442
01:48:49,875 --> 01:48:52,836
- I want them looking like mirrors. - Yes, sir.

1443
01:48:58,342 --> 01:48:59,802
It's good having you back.

1444
01:49:00,719 --> 01:49:02,596
Place wasn't the same without you.

1445
01:49:49,476 --> 01:49:50,561
Lights out!

1446
01:50:23,384 --> 01:50:25,929
I've had some long nights in stir.

1447
01:50:26,472 --> 01:50:29,099
Alone in the dark with nothing but your thoughts...

1448
01:50:29,307 --> 01:50:31,977
...time can draw out like a blade.

1449
01:50:37,316 --> 01:50:40,319
That was the longest night of my life.

1450
01:50:56,167 --> 01:50:57,335
Give me a count!

1451
01:51:01,840 --> 01:51:04,675
Tier 3 south, clear!

1452
01:51:07,845 --> 01:51:10,974
Man missing on tier 2, cell 245!

1453
01:51:11,182 --> 01:51:12,267
Dufresne!

1454
01:51:12,809 --> 01:51:15,228
Come out. You're holding up the show!

1455
01:51:18,357 --> 01:51:21,777
Don't make me come down or I'll thump your skull for you!

1456
01:51:29,367 --> 01:51:33,205
Damn it, you're putting me behind! I got a schedule to keep.

1457
01:51:33,372 --> 01:51:36,667
You'd better be sick or dead in there. I shit you not!

1458
01:51:36,874 --> 01:51:38,209
You hear me?

1459
01:51:44,341 --> 01:51:46,426
Oh, my Holy God.

1460
01:52:03,402 --> 01:52:05,987
I want every man on this cellblock questioned.

1461
01:52:06,404 --> 01:52:08,990
- Start with that friend of his. - Who?

1462
01:52:11,576 --> 01:52:13,119
Open 237.

1463
01:52:16,622 --> 01:52:20,293
What do you mean, "He just wasn't here"? Don't say that to me.

1464
01:52:21,170 --> 01:52:22,503
Don't tell me that again.

1465
01:52:22,712 --> 01:52:23,713
But sir, he wasn't.

1466
01:52:23,963 --> 01:52:27,425
I can see that, Haig! Think I'm blind?

1467
01:52:28,092 --> 01:52:29,510
Is that what you're saying?

1468
01:52:29,719 --> 01:52:32,388
- Am I blind, Haig? - No, sir!

1469
01:52:33,264 --> 01:52:35,767
What about you. You blind?

1470
01:52:36,267 --> 01:52:38,853
- Tell me what this is. - Last night's count.

1471
01:52:39,061 --> 01:52:42,356
You see Dufresne's name there? I sure do. Right there.

1472
01:52:42,565 --> 01:52:43,900
"Dufresne."

1473
01:52:45,568 --> 01:52:47,027
He was in his cell...

1474
01:52:47,278 --> 01:52:48,237
...at lights out.

1475
01:52:48,446 --> 01:52:50,698
Reasonable he'd be here in the morning.

1476
01:52:51,741 --> 01:52:53,366
I want him found.

1477
01:52:53,576 --> 01:52:57,038
Not tomorrow, not after breakfast. Now!

1478
01:52:57,287 --> 01:52:58,456
Yes, sir.

1479
01:52:59,456 --> 01:53:01,750
Let's go. Move your butts.

1480
01:53:05,129 --> 01:53:06,130
Stand.

1481
01:53:07,590 --> 01:53:08,633
Well?

1482
01:53:12,095 --> 01:53:13,054
Well, what?

1483
01:53:13,303 --> 01:53:16,765
I see you two all the time. You're thick as thieves, you are.

1484
01:53:17,808 --> 01:53:19,310
He must have said something.

1485
01:53:20,061 --> 01:53:21,604
No, sir, Warden.

1486
01:53:21,978 --> 01:53:22,939
Not a word.

1487
01:53:24,774 --> 01:53:27,317
Lord, it's a miracle!

1488
01:53:27,944 --> 01:53:30,487
Man vanished like a fart in the wind.

1489
01:53:31,155 --> 01:53:32,323
Nothing left...

1490
01:53:33,157 --> 01:53:35,910
...but some damn rocks on a windowsill.

1491
01:53:36,118 --> 01:53:38,830
And that cupcake on the wall. Let's ask her.

1492
01:53:39,037 --> 01:53:40,790
Maybe she knows.

1493
01:53:41,039 --> 01:53:43,792
What say there, fuzzy-britches? Feel like talking?

1494
01:53:45,210 --> 01:53:46,503
Guess not.

1495
01:53:47,087 --> 01:53:49,297
Why should she be any different?

1496
01:53:50,341 --> 01:53:52,008
This is a conspiracy.

1497
01:53:52,677 --> 01:53:54,469
That's what this is.

1498
01:53:55,096 --> 01:53:58,807
One big, damn conspiracy!

1499
01:53:59,099 --> 01:54:01,101
And everyone's in on it!

1500
01:54:01,726 --> 01:54:02,895
Including her!

1501
01:54:37,929 --> 01:54:39,639
In 1966...

1502
01:54:39,848 --> 01:54:42,226
...Andy Dufresne escaped...

1503
01:54:42,434 --> 01:54:44,019
...from Shawshank Prison.

1504
01:54:46,229 --> 01:54:48,815
All they found was a muddy set of prison clothes...

1505
01:54:49,442 --> 01:54:50,984
...a bar of soap...

1506
01:54:51,193 --> 01:54:52,736
...and an old rock hammer...

1507
01:54:52,944 --> 01:54:55,405
...damn near worn down to the nub.

1508
01:54:59,910 --> 01:55:04,706
I had thought it'd take a man 600 years to tunnel through the wall with it.

1509
01:55:05,333 --> 01:55:07,959
Old Andy did it in less than 20.

1510
01:55:26,270 --> 01:55:28,563
Oh, Andy loved geology.

1511
01:55:29,314 --> 01:55:32,485
I imagine it appealed to his meticulous nature.

1512
01:55:32,901 --> 01:55:34,736
An ice age here...

1513
01:55:35,153 --> 01:55:37,656
...million years of mountain building there.

1514
01:55:38,657 --> 01:55:41,493
Geology is the study of pressure and time.

1515
01:55:42,327 --> 01:55:44,328
That's all it takes, really.

1516
01:55:45,080 --> 01:55:46,164
Pressure...

1517
01:55:46,790 --> 01:55:48,082
...and time.

1518
01:55:49,418 --> 01:55:51,461
That and a big goddamn poster.

1519
01:55:55,507 --> 01:55:56,465
Like I said...

1520
01:55:56,967 --> 01:56:00,928
...in prison, a man will do anything to keep his mind occupied.

1521
01:56:03,765 --> 01:56:08,562
Seems Andy's favorite hobby was toting his wall out into the exercise yard...

1522
01:56:09,104 --> 01:56:11,022
...a handful at a time.

1523
01:56:12,315 --> 01:56:14,401
I guess after Tommy was killed...

1524
01:56:14,609 --> 01:56:17,571
...Andy decided he'd been here long enough.

1525
01:56:17,779 --> 01:56:20,323
Lickety-split. I want to get home.

1526
01:56:21,074 --> 01:56:23,118
I'm just about finished, sir.

1527
01:56:44,555 --> 01:56:46,516
Three deposits tonight.

1528
01:56:53,814 --> 01:56:56,108
Andy did like he was told.

1529
01:56:56,318 --> 01:56:59,780
Buffed those shoes to a high mirror-shine.

1530
01:57:03,491 --> 01:57:05,785
The guards simply didn't notice.

1531
01:57:06,076 --> 01:57:07,536
Neither did I.

1532
01:57:07,746 --> 01:57:09,288
I mean, seriously...

1533
01:57:09,498 --> 01:57:12,501
...how often do you really look at a man's shoes?

1534
01:59:16,040 --> 01:59:19,210
Andy crawled to freedom through 500 yards...

1535
01:59:19,459 --> 01:59:22,796
...of shit-smelling foulness I can't even imagine.

1536
01:59:23,797 --> 01:59:26,132
Or maybe I just don't want to.

1537
01:59:30,513 --> 01:59:32,889
Five hundred yards.

1538
01:59:33,390 --> 01:59:36,477
That's the length of five football fields.

1539
01:59:37,102 --> 01:59:39,647
Just shy of half a mile.

1540
02:00:34,909 --> 02:00:39,497
The next morning, right about the time Raquel was spilling her secret...

1541
02:00:39,789 --> 02:00:42,625
...a man nobody ever laid eyes on before...

1542
02:00:42,877 --> 02:00:45,462
...strolled into the Maine National Bank.

1543
02:00:46,046 --> 02:00:49,215
Until that moment, he didn't exist.

1544
02:00:49,758 --> 02:00:52,136
- Except on paper. - May I help you?

1545
02:00:52,553 --> 02:00:54,471
He had all the proper ID...

1546
02:00:54,679 --> 02:00:58,391
...driver's license, birth certificate, Social Security card...

1547
02:00:58,808 --> 02:01:01,352
And the signature was a spot-on match.

1548
02:01:01,561 --> 02:01:04,647
I must say I'm sorry to be losing your business.

1549
02:01:04,856 --> 02:01:06,566
I hope you'll enjoy living abroad.

1550
02:01:07,400 --> 02:01:08,485
Thank you.

1551
02:01:09,152 --> 02:01:10,320
I'm sure I will.

1552
02:01:11,237 --> 02:01:14,574
Here's your cashier's check, sir. Will there be anything else?

1553
02:01:14,783 --> 02:01:15,742
Please.

1554
02:01:16,326 --> 02:01:18,828
Would you add this to your outgoing mail?

1555
02:01:19,329 --> 02:01:20,538
I'd be happy to.

1556
02:01:22,332 --> 02:01:23,666
Good day, sir.

1557
02:01:24,626 --> 02:01:29,047
Mr. Stevens visited nearly a dozen banks in the Portland area that morning.

1558
02:01:29,506 --> 02:01:31,091
All told, he blew town...

1559
02:01:31,299 --> 02:01:36,137
...with better than $370,000 of Warden Norton's money.

1560
02:01:36,846 --> 02:01:39,599
Severance pay for 19 years.

1561
02:01:46,856 --> 02:01:49,067
Good morning, Portland Daily Bugle.

1562
02:02:41,910 --> 02:02:44,873
Byron Hadley? You have the right to remain silent.

1563
02:02:45,123 --> 02:02:49,543
If you give up this right, anything you say can be held against you in court.

1564
02:02:50,002 --> 02:02:54,465
I wasn't there to see, but I hear Byron Hadley sobbed like a girl...

1565
02:02:54,674 --> 02:02:56,134
...when they took him away.

1566
02:03:02,056 --> 02:03:05,434
Norton had no intention of going that quietly.

1567
02:03:21,284 --> 02:03:22,118
Samuel Norton.

1568
02:03:22,910 --> 02:03:25,370
We have a warrant for your arrest. Open up.

1569
02:03:30,085 --> 02:03:31,210
Open the door.

1570
02:03:31,752 --> 02:03:33,003
I'm not sure which key.

1571
02:03:43,138 --> 02:03:45,100
Make it easy on yourself, Norton!

1572
02:03:56,610 --> 02:04:00,030
I like to think the last thing that went through his head...

1573
02:04:00,280 --> 02:04:01,866
...other than that bullet...

1574
02:04:02,074 --> 02:04:06,495
...was to wonder how the hell Andy Dufresne ever got the best of him.

1575
02:04:08,997 --> 02:04:12,459
Not long after the warden deprived us of his company...

1576
02:04:12,751 --> 02:04:14,795
...I got a postcard in the mail.

1577
02:04:16,297 --> 02:04:19,091
It was blank, but the postmark said...

1578
02:04:19,299 --> 02:04:21,427
...Fort Hancock, Texas.

1579
02:04:21,969 --> 02:04:23,345
Fort Hancock...

1580
02:04:23,637 --> 02:04:25,347
...right on the border.

1581
02:04:25,764 --> 02:04:27,724
That's where Andy crossed.

1582
02:04:28,308 --> 02:04:32,438
When I picture him heading south in his own car with the top down...

1583
02:04:32,813 --> 02:04:35,023
...it always makes me laugh.

1584
02:04:36,859 --> 02:04:38,444
Andy Dufresne...

1585
02:04:38,819 --> 02:04:40,821
...who crawled through a river of shit...

1586
02:04:41,029 --> 02:04:43,449
...and came out clean on the other side.

1587
02:04:44,283 --> 02:04:45,616
Andy Dufresne...

1588
02:04:46,452 --> 02:04:48,036
...headed for the Pacific.

1589
02:04:50,998 --> 02:04:53,167
Hadley's got him by the throat, right?

1590
02:04:53,375 --> 02:04:57,170
He says, "I believe this boy's about to have himself an accident."

1591
02:04:57,379 --> 02:05:00,257
Those of us who knew him best talk about him often.

1592
02:05:00,923 --> 02:05:02,634
I swear, the stuff he pulled...

1593
02:05:02,843 --> 02:05:05,804
"My friends could use a couple of beers."

1594
02:05:06,137 --> 02:05:07,806
And he got it!

1595
02:05:09,015 --> 02:05:11,435
Sometimes it makes me sad, though...

1596
02:05:11,685 --> 02:05:13,145
...Andy being gone.

1597
02:05:13,854 --> 02:05:17,982
I have to remind myself that some birds aren't meant to be caged.

1598
02:05:18,442 --> 02:05:20,486
Their feathers are just too bright.

1599
02:05:22,196 --> 02:05:23,947
And when they fly away...

1600
02:05:24,364 --> 02:05:28,786
...the part of you that knows it was a sin to lock them up does rejoice.

1601
02:05:29,870 --> 02:05:31,120
But still...

1602
02:05:31,579 --> 02:05:35,918
...the place you live in is that much more drab and empty that they're gone.

1603
02:05:40,130 --> 02:05:42,507
I guess I just miss my friend.

1604
02:06:07,198 --> 02:06:08,116
Please sit down.

1605
02:06:13,956 --> 02:06:15,541
Ellis Boyd Redding...

1606
02:06:16,082 --> 02:06:19,335
...your files say you've served 40 years of a life sentence.

1607
02:06:20,128 --> 02:06:22,255
You feel you've been rehabilitated?

1608
02:06:24,132 --> 02:06:25,716
Rehabilitated?

1609
02:06:27,760 --> 02:06:29,637
Well, now, let me see.

1610
02:06:30,638 --> 02:06:32,974
I don't have any idea what that means.

1611
02:06:35,101 --> 02:06:37,354
It means you're ready to rejoin society-

1612
02:06:37,938 --> 02:06:40,232
I know what you think it means, sonny.

1613
02:06:42,442 --> 02:06:44,402
To me it's just a made-up word.

1614
02:06:45,778 --> 02:06:48,072
A politician's word so that...

1615
02:06:48,531 --> 02:06:52,077
...young fellas like yourself can wear a suit and a tie...

1616
02:06:52,535 --> 02:06:54,120
...and have a job.

1617
02:06:56,123 --> 02:06:58,207
What do you really want to know?

1618
02:06:58,958 --> 02:07:00,960
Am I sorry for what I did?

1619
02:07:01,377 --> 02:07:02,545
Well, are you?

1620
02:07:04,464 --> 02:07:07,258
There's not a day goes by I don't feel regret.

1621
02:07:08,634 --> 02:07:12,096
Not because I'm in here or because you think I should.

1622
02:07:15,600 --> 02:07:18,144
I look back on the way I was then...

1623
02:07:20,313 --> 02:07:21,439
...a young...

1624
02:07:22,565 --> 02:07:26,068
...stupid kid who committed that terrible crime.

1625
02:07:29,071 --> 02:07:30,782
I want to talk to him.

1626
02:07:32,700 --> 02:07:35,161
I want to try and talk some sense to him.

1627
02:07:35,912 --> 02:07:37,830
Tell him the way things are.

1628
02:07:39,957 --> 02:07:41,375
But I can't.

1629
02:07:43,586 --> 02:07:45,588
That kid's long gone...

1630
02:07:46,798 --> 02:07:49,550
...and this old man is all that's left.

1631
02:07:50,968 --> 02:07:52,428
I got to live with that.

1632
02:07:53,805 --> 02:07:55,473
Rehabilitated?

1633
02:07:55,848 --> 02:07:57,683
It's just a bullshit word.

1634
02:07:58,643 --> 02:08:03,397
So you go on and stamp your forms, sonny, and stop wasting my time.

1635
02:08:04,649 --> 02:08:06,900
Because to tell you the truth...

1636
02:08:07,235 --> 02:08:09,237
...I don't give a shit.

1637
02:09:35,781 --> 02:09:37,157
Here you go, miss.

1638
02:09:40,118 --> 02:09:41,119
Restroom break?

1639
02:09:46,958 --> 02:09:51,463
You don't need to ask me every time you need to go take a piss. Just go.

1640
02:10:04,518 --> 02:10:07,604
Forty years I've been asking permission to piss.

1641
02:10:08,438 --> 02:10:11,399
I can't squeeze a drop without say-so.

1642
02:10:14,945 --> 02:10:17,114
There's a harsh truth to face.

1643
02:10:18,782 --> 02:10:21,660
No way I'm going to make it on the outside.

1644
02:10:29,543 --> 02:10:33,255
All I do anymore is think of ways to break my parole...

1645
02:10:34,047 --> 02:10:36,508
...so maybe they'd send me back.

1646
02:10:41,721 --> 02:10:44,182
Terrible thing, to live in fear.

1647
02:10:45,058 --> 02:10:46,725
Brooks Hatlen knew it.

1648
02:10:46,935 --> 02:10:48,854
Knew it all too well.

1649
02:10:50,479 --> 02:10:53,859
All I want is to be back where things make sense.

1650
02:10:54,317 --> 02:10:57,028
Where I won't have to be afraid all the time.

1651
02:10:59,030 --> 02:11:01,408
Only one thing stops me.

1652
02:11:02,868 --> 02:11:05,245
A promise I made to Andy.

1653
02:11:25,766 --> 02:11:26,891
There it is.

1654
02:11:37,527 --> 02:11:39,196
Much obliged, sir.

1655
02:15:18,496 --> 02:15:19,749
Dear Red:

1656
02:15:19,999 --> 02:15:22,460
If you're reading this, you've gotten out...

1657
02:15:22,667 --> 02:15:26,755
...and if you've come this far, maybe you'd come a bit further.

1658
02:15:27,005 --> 02:15:29,466
You remember the name of the town, don't you?

1659
02:15:31,801 --> 02:15:33,261
Zihuatanejo.

1660
02:15:34,929 --> 02:15:38,517
I could use a good man to help me get my project on wheels.

1661
02:15:39,017 --> 02:15:42,563
I'll keep an eye out for you, and the chessboard ready.

1662
02:15:43,354 --> 02:15:44,773
Remember, Red...

1663
02:15:45,106 --> 02:15:47,192
...hope is a good thing...

1664
02:15:47,400 --> 02:15:49,402
...maybe the best of things.

1665
02:15:49,612 --> 02:15:51,781
And no good thing ever dies.

1666
02:15:52,322 --> 02:15:55,158
I will be hoping that this letter finds you...

1667
02:15:55,366 --> 02:15:57,118
...and finds you well.

1668
02:15:57,619 --> 02:15:58,828
Your friend...

1669
02:15:59,037 --> 02:16:00,205
...Andy.

1670
02:16:34,030 --> 02:16:35,781
"Get busy living...

1671
02:16:35,990 --> 02:16:37,866
...or get busy dying."

1672
02:16:40,577 --> 02:16:42,746
That's goddamn right.

1673
02:16:48,002 --> 02:16:50,129
For the second time in my life...

1674
02:16:50,379 --> 02:16:52,924
...I'm guilty of committing a crime.

1675
02:16:54,008 --> 02:16:55,925
Parole violation.

1676
02:16:56,593 --> 02:17:00,263
Of course I doubt they'll toss up any roadblocks for that.

1677
02:17:00,932 --> 02:17:02,976
Not for an old crook like me.

1678
02:17:03,183 --> 02:17:05,186
Fort Hancock, Texas, please.

1679
02:17:08,897 --> 02:17:13,611
I find I'm so excited I can barely sit still or hold a thought in my head.

1680
02:17:14,070 --> 02:17:17,365
I think it's the excitement only a free man can feel.

1681
02:17:17,573 --> 02:17:20,075
A free man at the start of a long journey...

1682
02:17:20,285 --> 02:17:22,870
...whose conclusion is uncertain.

1683
02:17:25,915 --> 02:17:28,541
I hope I can make it across the border.

1684
02:17:30,085 --> 02:17:33,089
I hope to see my friend and shake his hand.

1685
02:17:34,966 --> 02:17:39,053
I hope the Pacific is as blue as it has been in my dreams.

1686
02:17:40,888 --> 02:17:42,056
I hope.
